// scripts/battle-maps.js - written by scripts/tools/make-battle-maps.js. Do not edit. Read by generator.html: the battle
// profiles' "How it was fought" (profile name: [slug, version of data/battles/<slug>.json, [[phase title, text]]]).
var BATTLE_MAPS = {
  "Battle of Actium": ["battle-of-actium","95dea998f9",[["Antony's fleet comes out and forms line","Octavian's fleet waited beyond the straits. Antony led his ships out through the straits and they formed a line, with Cleopatra's squadron behind them, and stayed quiet."],["Octavian steers to the right","After a short hesitation Octavian ordered his ships to steer to the right and pass the enemy's ships. Fearing that his line would be surrounded, Antony was forced to give the order to attack."],["Sosius attacks and Octavian's left stays out of range","Sosius opened the attack from Antony's left wing. Antony hoped to drive back Agrippa's wing at the north end of his line with his biggest ships, but Octavian's fleet stayed out of range."],["The fleets fight through the afternoon","The two lines closed after midday and fought on all afternoon. Antony's larger ships rained missiles on the smaller ones, which closed in, shot at the crews and drew off again, and neither side gained a clear result."],["Cleopatra's squadron sails for the open sea","Cleopatra's squadron, in the rear, took no part in the fighting and retreated to the open sea. A breeze sprang up in the right direction and the ships were soon out of sight."],["Antony follows and the rest are taken","Antony thought all was lost and followed the fleeing squadron. He moved to a smaller vessel and slipped away with a few ships as escort, and the ships left behind were captured or sunk."]]],
  "Battle of Adrianople": ["battle-of-adrianople","28fbf4981d",[["The armies face each other below the hill","The Goths held a wagon circle on a hilltop, and the Romans, tired and thirsty after a march, came up facing it. Both sides drew up in battle order, and the Gothic cavalry was away."],["The shield-archers attack without orders","Units of the imperial guard began the fight on their own and attacked the Goths without support. They were easily pushed back."],["The left wing reaches the wagons","The shield-archers fell back while the Roman left wing pressed on until it reached the circle of wagons. By then, the accounts say, it was too late."],["The Gothic cavalry returns and surrounds the Romans","The Gothic cavalry came back from foraging and arrived to support the infantry. It surrounded the Roman troops, who were already in disarray after the failed assault."],["The Romans are driven back and routed","The Romans retreated to the base of the hill, where they could not manoeuvre in their heavy armour. Casualties and exhaustion led to a rout, and the cavalry kept up the attack until nightfall."]]],
  "Battle of Adwa": ["battle-of-adwa","faf629de75",[["The Italian columns march out in parallel","Baratieri's columns advanced towards the Ethiopian positions to reach the crests of three mountains, Albertone on the left, Arimondi in the centre and Dabormida on the right, with Ellena's reserve following behind Arimondi."],["Albertone loses his line and is charged","Albertone's column went astray and he found himself cut off from the rest of the army. His vanguard was then ferociously charged by Ethiopian units coming down from the mountains."],["Dabormida is cut off in the valley","Dabormida pushed his brigade into the valley of Mariam Shewito to relieve Albertone, away from where it was meant to go. A gap of about two miles opened, and Ras Makonnen advanced into it."],["Menelik's reserve falls on Albertone, who is routed","Menelik sent his guard against Albertone's front while Taytu's troops and Ras Mikael's troops attacked the flanks. The brigade, outflanked, began a fighting retreat that turned into a rout."],["The Ethiopians split and fall on Arimondi","After the rout the Ethiopian columns came down on Arimondi's position in two parts. One struck Mount Belah on the Italian right, and the other the junction on the Italian left, held by the 3rd Native Battalion, detached from Ellena."],["The centre gives way and Baratieri orders a retreat","Pressed at the front and outflanked on both sides, Arimondi's brigade gave way. Baratieri ordered a general retreat towards Adi Ugri and Adi Keyh, and Ras Mikael's Oromo cavalry cut down the retreating Italians."],["Dabormida's brigade is encircled","Ethiopian troops that had beaten the other brigades moved into the valley. Dabormida's final attack was repulsed with heavy losses, and as the brigade tried to withdraw it found itself effectively encircled."]]],
  "Battle of Agincourt": ["battle-of-agincourt","77e5417dcf",[["The two armies are drawn up","The English stood in three groups across the defile, with archers on the flanks and men-at-arms in the centre. The French stood in a vanguard and a main battle behind it, with dismounted men-at-arms in the wings, cavalry on the flanks of the vanguard and a mounted rearguard."],["The English move up and shoot first","Henry ordered his army forward and the stakes were pulled up and set again in the new position. The English longbowmen then shot from extreme range, and the French cavalry charged only after this first volley."],["The French cavalry charge the archers","The French cavalry, disorganised and not at full numbers, charged towards the longbowmen. They could not outflank the archers because of the woodland, and could not charge through the stakes."],["The wounded horses break back through the French","The charge failed and the cavalry fell back. Wounded and panicking horses galloped through the advancing infantry, scattering and trampling it, while the vanguard walked on through the mud."],["The French men-at-arms reach the English line","The surviving French men-at-arms reached the front of the English line and pushed it back, while the longbowmen on the flanks went on shooting at close range. When their arrows ran out the archers attacked the French with hatchets, swords, spears and mallets."],["The second line joins and the fight is lost in the press","The French second line joined the attack but was swallowed up, the narrow ground hampering it. The fighting lasted about three hours, until the leaders of the second line were killed or captured, as those of the first line had been."]]],
  "Battle of Ain Jalut": ["battle-of-ain-jalut","8b552b33a3",[["The Mongols hold the spring","Kitbuqa reached Ain Jalut first and camped by the spring, with Mount Gilboa covering one flank. Baibars, leading the Mamluk vanguard, sighted the camp from a height, while Qutuz hid the bulk of his army in the highlands."],["The Mongols advance and Baibars strikes and runs","The Mongols moved first. For many hours Baibars provoked them with hit-and-run attacks and kept most of his men out of the fight."],["The feigned retreat","After another heavy Mongol assault Baibars and his men pretended to break and rode for the highlands. Kitbuqa did not suspect a trick and followed with all his troops."],["The trap closes","When the Mongols reached the highlands the hidden Mamluks came out, shooting arrows and charging with their cavalry. The Mongols found themselves surrounded."],["The Mongols break the left, and Qutuz charges","The Mongols fought hard to break out and nearly destroyed the Mamluk left wing. Qutuz threw off his helmet so that his men could see him and led his own unit towards the weakened side."],["Flight towards Baysan","The Mongols were pushed back and fled towards Baysan with Qutuz’s men behind them. They rallied and counter-attacked, but the fight turned against them and Kitbuqa was killed with most of his army."]]],
  "Battle of Alarcos": ["battle-of-alarcos","33b4901b27",[["The two armies form up","The Almohads formed around the hill of La Cabeza in a vanguard and a rearguard. The Castilian king placed his heavy cavalry in one compact group and kept the infantry and Military Orders to follow."],["The Castilian cavalry charges down","Alfonso chose to move first. His forces came down from their high position and the heavy cavalry charged across the lower plain into the front lines of the Muslims."],["The front line is scattered and the vizier killed","In a three hour engagement the knights first dispersed the Bani Marin and the Zenatas, then attacked the Hintata and killed the vizier Abu Yahya."],["The Andalusi forces are routed","The knights then struggled against the al-Andalus forces of Ibn Sanadid and routed them, but they were fatigued and men were falling."],["The Almohad flank and rearguard surround the knights","As the Christians tired and fell, the Arab left under Yarmun enveloped their flank and rear. The Almohad rearguard under al-Mansur then attacked and surrounded the knights."],["Alfonso commits the remaining forces","Alfonso advanced with all his remaining forces into the melee and was assaulted from all sides under a rain of arrows. His bodyguard removed him from the action and they fled to Toledo."],["The infantry is destroyed and the knights reach Alarcos","The Castilian infantry was destroyed together with most of the Orders. López de Haro tried to break through the ring and took refuge in the unfinished fortress of Alarcos with a fraction of his knights."]]],
  "Battle of Alesia": ["battle-of-alesia","aef0eac112",[["Two lines of fortification round Alesia","Caesar shut the Gauls into Alesia and built two lines of works, one facing the town and one facing outwards against a relief force. The Roman lines, with a camp of two legions on a hill beside them, stood ready."],["The relief force and the besieged attack together","The relief force arrived and attacked the outer line while the besieged Gauls attacked the inner line at the same moment. The combined attack failed."],["A night attack fails and the Gauls withdraw","The Gauls attacked again at night. The relief force withdrew at first light for fear of being surrounded, and the besieged, delayed by filling the Roman trenches, returned to the town."],["Sixty thousand Gauls attack the weak camp","The Gauls chose a weak point where a camp of two legions stood on bad ground and sent 60,000 men against it, attacking at noon. Vercingetorix sallied against the inner line at the same time."],["Labienus and Caesar come to the weak point","Caesar sent Labienus to support the weak area, then led fresh troops there himself. The attack on the inner line was repelled."],["Roman cavalry takes the Gauls in the rear","Part of the Roman cavalry left the outer line and appeared at the rear of the Gauls. The Roman troops advanced rapidly, the Gauls fled, and the cavalry intercepted them. The besieged Gauls were pulled back from the fortification."]]],
  "Battle of Austerlitz": ["battle-of-austerlitz","4f2b6af390",[["The armies take their ground","Napoleon abandoned the Pratzen Heights and moved all his troops but the right flank into dead ground. The Allies planned four columns against the French right, with the Russian Guard in reserve and Bagration on their right."],["The Allied columns attack the French right","The first Allied lines attacked Telnitz and drove the French across the Goldbach. The first men of Davout's corps arrived and threw the Allies out of the village, and French artillery checked further attacks."],["The Tsar orders Kutuzov off the Pratzen Heights","Kutuzov's corps had halted on the Heights, but the Tsar ordered it to withdraw. That left the Allied centre weak."],["Soult's corps climbs the Heights","As the mist cleared, Napoleon ordered the attack. Soult's corps went up the slope and the Allies sent the delayed detachments of the fourth column into the fight. After over an hour of fighting the French bayoneted the Allies off the Heights."],["The Russian Guard counterattacks and breaks","Grand Duke Constantine's Guard counterattacked in Vandamme's section and forced a bloody effort. French Guard cavalry and Bernadotte's I Corps came up, and the Russians broke and were pursued for about a quarter of a mile."],["In the north the French drive off the cavalry and Bagration","Liechtenstein's heavy cavalry attacked Murat's lighter cavalry, and French cuirassiers finished off the Russian cavalry after a long and bitter mêlée. Lannes then led his corps against Bagration and drove him off the field."],["Sokolnitz falls and the Allied army leaves the field","St. Hilaire's division and part of Davout's corps broke through at Sokolnitz. The commanders of the first two columns fled, and general panic seized the Allied army, which abandoned the field in all possible directions."]]],
  "Battle of Ayacucho": ["battle-of-ayacucho","138640c4de",[["The armies face each other","The independentist line formed an angle, with Córdova on the right, La Mar on the left, Miller's cavalry in the centre and Lara in reserve. The royalists stood on the heights of Condorcunca, with Valdés' vanguard on their right."],["The royalists come down the slope","Canterac's plan sent Valdés across the Pampas to turn Sucre's left while the rest of the army descended frontally from the hill. The royalists moved onto an exposed slope, and Córdova's division, supported by Miller's cavalry, fired on them."],["Villalobos is pushed back and Monet is surrounded","Córdova's fire pushed back Villalobos' scattered men. Monet crossed the ravine without waiting for his cavalry, formed two battalions and was surrounded by the independentist division before the rest of his troops could form."],["Monet's division breaks and Ferraz charges","Monet was wounded and his scattered divisions dragged the militia with them. Ferraz's cavalry charged the squadrons pursuing Monet's broken left, but crossfire from the infantry caused heavy casualties."],["Valdés attacks the isolated building","On the other flank, La Mar and Lara stopped the assault of Valdés' veterans, who had gone forward to take the isolated building. The independentists were reinforced and attacked again, helped by Córdova's division."],["The reserve scatters and the viceroy is taken","Canterac led the reserve across the plain, but its recruits scattered before they met the enemy. By one o'clock the viceroy had been wounded and made prisoner. Valdés retreated to the hill held by his rearguard."]]],
  "Battle of Badr": ["battle-of-badr","95cf3c807e",[["The armies face each other across the valley","The Quraysh were encamped in the south-eastern part of the valley near the road from Mecca. The Muslims had come from the north and held the well nearest the Meccans."],["Duels before the lines meet","Men came out from each side to fight singly. Hamza, Ubayda and Ali met three Meccans who came out of the Quraysh ranks. Ali and Hamza each killed an opponent quickly."],["The Meccans charge","A shower of arrows from both sides followed the duels. The Meccans then took the offensive and charged upon the Muslim lines, and the Muslim duellists were carried back into the lines."],["The Muslims counter-attack","Muhammad gave the order for a counter-attack and the Muslim army rushed the Quraysh lines. Both Muslim battalions moved forward against the Meccan body."],["The Meccan lines break","The Meccans broke and ran. The fighting lasted a few hours and was over by early afternoon, after the Meccan army had scattered."]]],
  "Battle of Boyacá": ["battle-of-boyaca","8d6178e716",[["The vanguards meet at Casa de Teja","The Royalist vanguard had stopped near Casa de Teja to rest. Patriot skirmishers met it, and Santander ordered the Cazadores Battalion to attack the Royalists there."],["The Royalist vanguard falls back over the bridge","The Royalist vanguard crossed the bridge over the Teatinos River and took defensive positions there. Santander's full vanguard reached Casa de Teja, and he sent Bejar down the river to cross it and attack the Royalists from the rear."],["The Royalist rearguard is cut off","The Royalist rearguard was still several kilometres behind, and Anzoátegui blocked the way between it and the vanguard. Outnumbered, it retreated to a small hill and formed with its guns in the centre, infantry beside them and cavalry on the wings."],["Flank attacks on the Royalist rearguard","Anzoátegui ordered a flank attack: Barcelona and Bravos de Paez on the right and the British Legion and Rifles on the left. Arthur Sandes led the Rifles against the Royalist guns, and only one of the three could be put together before its carriage broke."],["Lancers break the Royalist rearguard","Bolívar ordered lancers against the centre of the Royalist infantry. The Mounted Grenadiers ran towards Samaca and the dragoons turned back, and panic spread among the infantry, which did not carry out its order to charge with the bayonet."],["Santander storms the bridge","Bejar's detachment forded the river and came up on the rear of the Royalist vanguard, which it engaged. Santander led a bayonet charge across the bridge, and the Royalist vanguard fled, leaving its commander Colonel Juan Tolrá dead on the bridge."]]],
  "Battle of Cannae": ["battle-of-cannae","2bca6e19d5",[["The armies draw up facing each other","The Romans faced east with the Aufidus on their right and massed their infantry deep in the centre. Hannibal put the Hispanic and Gallic infantry in the middle, the African infantry at the ends of the infantry line and the cavalry on the flanks."],["The cavalry clash and the centres advance","The battle opened with a fierce cavalry fight on both flanks. In the centre Hannibal moved the Hispanic and Gallic infantry forward in a curve, keeping the Africans back."],["The Roman cavalry is driven off","The Carthaginian horse got the upper hand on the Roman right. Hasdrubal did not chase them but rode to the other side of the field, and the allied cavalry broke between him and the Numidians."],["The centre gives way step by step","The Roman heavy infantry pressed forward and Hannibal held his men together in a controlled retreat. The curve buckled inwards as the Romans advanced and reserve lines pushed into the gaps."],["The Africans turn in and the cavalry takes the rear","The Carthaginian cavalry attacked the Roman centre from behind. Hannibal then ordered the African infantry to turn inwards against the Roman flanks, which formed a pincer."],["The Romans are enclosed and killed","Enclosed in a pocket with no means of escape, the Roman infantry was cut down by the cavalry behind it, the African infantry on both sides and the centre in front."]]],
  "Battle of Caporetto": ["battle-of-caporetto","031710916e",[["The attackers gather east of the Italian line","The German and Austro-Hungarian plan sent von Below's army against the Caporetto sector, with Boroević's army attacking the eastern end of the salient and ground near the Adriatic. The Italian Second Army held the line, with the IV and XXVII Corps on either side of the valley."],["Gas and the bombardment","At 02:00 gas projectors fired near Bovec, and the defenders fled their trenches. At 06:41 2,200 guns opened fire on the front."],["The infantry break the line in the valley","After two large mines were detonated under strong points on the heights, the infantry attacked and breached the line between the IV and XXVII Corps. Alpine troops went along the ridges on either side to protect the attackers' flanks."],["The central column drives down the valley road","The attackers in the valley marched almost unopposed along the road, and some advanced 25 kilometres on the first day. The Italians beat back the attackers on either side, but the central penetration threw their whole army into disarray."],["The Italian retreat to the Tagliamento","Cadorna ordered most of the Italian force to retreat across the Tagliamento, and it took four days to cross. The Central Powers followed closely and ambushed the defenders whenever they could."],["A bridgehead over the Tagliamento","After an attack by a battalion of the 4th Bosnian Infantry Regiment, the 55th Infantry Division established a bridgehead across the Tagliamento. The rapid success had stretched German and Austro-Hungarian supply lines to the breaking point."],["The Italians stand on the Piave","The Central Powers could not launch another attack to cut off part of the Italian army against the Adriatic. Cadorna retreated further and by 10 November held a position on the Piave River and Monte Grappa."]]],
  "Battle of Carrhae": ["battle-of-carrhae","bce7a41b6d",[["The Romans form a hollow square","Crassus first agreed to a line with infantry in the centre and cavalry on the wings, then redeployed his men into a hollow square. The Parthian cataphracts advanced, their armour covered in cloths."],["Horse archers surround the square","Surena sent his horse archers round the Roman square, beyond the range of the pila. Crassus sent his skirmishers out to drive them off, and the archers' arrows drove the skirmishers back."],["The cataphracts charge the testudo","The legionaries locked their shields in the testudo, which limited their fighting in melee. The cataphracts repeatedly charged the line, and when the Romans loosened their formation the cataphracts drew back and the horse archers shot again."],["Publius is drawn away","Crassus sent his son Publius with Gallic cavalry, archers and eight cohorts to drive off the horse archers. They feigned retreat and drew Publius away from the main army, shooting at his force as it followed."],["Publius's force is cut off on a hill","Once Publius was well apart from the army, the cataphracts confronted his force and the horse archers cut off its retreat. The Gauls retreated to a hill, where Publius and his officers killed themselves. Only 500 Gauls were taken alive."],["The Romans advance and are attacked until nightfall","Crassus ordered a general advance and was shown his son's head on a spear. The horse archers shot at the Romans from all directions and the cataphracts charged in a series of attacks that went on until nightfall."],["The retreat towards Carrhae","Crassus ordered a disorganised retreat towards Carrhae, leaving 4,000 wounded behind. Four cohorts lost their way in the dark and were surrounded on a hill, where only 20 Romans survived."]]],
  "Battle of Crécy": ["battle-of-crecy","79198c2696",[["The English hold the hillside","Edward's army stood in three battles on a slope, with pits dug in front. French scouts came in sight of them from Abbeville, and the French column followed with the Genoese crossbowmen in front."],["The archery duel","The Genoese went forward and shot at the English longbowmen. The longbowmen outranged them and shot more than three times as fast, and the crossbowmen had no pavises, which were still with the French baggage."],["The Genoese flee through their own cavalry","The Italians were quickly defeated and fled. Knights in Alençon's division, hampered by the routed mercenaries, hacked at them as they retreated, and the clash threw the leading battle into disarray."],["Alençon's charge uphill","Alençon's battle charged, disordered by its hurried start, the fleeing Italians, the mud, the slope and the pits. Archers shot the unarmoured horses, and the charge had lost much of its force when it reached the English men-at-arms."],["Fresh French cavalry charge","The attack was beaten off, and Alençon was among those killed. A fresh body of French cavalry repeated the charge over ground littered with dead men and horses, and Edward sent a detachment from his reserve to help."],["The charges go on into the night","The French repeated their charges late into the night, each ending in fierce fighting and a French retreat. The English ranks were thinned, but men from the rear stepped forward to fill the gaps."],["Philip leaves the field","Philip was caught up in the fighting, had two horses killed under him and was hit by an arrow in the jaw. He left the field, though it is unclear why, and near midnight most of the French army melted away."]]],
  "Battle of Dien Bien Phu": ["battle-of-dien-bien-phu","710d8b3653",[["The French fortress and the Viet Minh ring","The French held a central headquarters with strong points round it, and Isabelle to the south. The Viet Minh moved divisions and artillery into the hills round the valley and the French were completely surrounded."],["Béatrice is overrun","The Viet Minh opened with a heavy bombardment of Béatrice, the northeastern outpost. The 312th Division then attacked, and by the early hours of 14 March the position had been taken."],["Gabrielle falls and Anne-Marie is given up","The 308th Division attacked Gabrielle on the night of 14 March, and the Algerian battalion fell back at 08:00 the next day. On 17 March most of the Tai troops left or defected, and Anne-Marie was abandoned."],["Attacks on the eastern and western positions","The 312th Division took Dominique 1 and 2 and the 316th Division took Eliane 1. In the west the 308th Division nearly broke through at Huguette 7, but the breach was sealed."],["Trenches and sapping","After heavy losses in open ground on 5 April, Giáp turned to entrenchment and sapping. The Viet Minh trenches almost surrounded Huguette 1 and 6, and Huguette 1 was overrun on 22 April."],["The eastern positions are overrun","On the night of 1 May the Viet Minh overran Eliane 1 and Dominique 3, and on 6 May they attacked Eliane 2 with Katyusha rockets. A mine under Eliane 2 was detonated and within a few hours the defenders were overrun."],["The last attack on the headquarters","Giáp ordered an all-out attack on the remaining French units. By evening enemy troops stood outside the headquarters bunker and the last radio report said the positions had been overrun."]]],
  "Battle of Gaugamela": ["battle-of-gaugamela","76f037d638",[["The two armies stand drawn up","The Persians were already on the field. Darius stood in the centre, Bessus commanded the left wing and Mazaeus the right, with chariots in front. Alexander held the right of the Macedonians and Parmenion the left, with the phalanx between them in a double line."],["The phalanx advances and Alexander rides to the right","Alexander ordered the infantry to march towards the centre of the enemy line, with the wings echeloned back. He himself rode towards the edge of the right flank with the Companions, to draw the Persian cavalry outwards."],["The Persian wings attack","The Scythian cavalry of the Persian left tried to get round Alexander's extreme right, and a long cavalry fight followed there. Darius also sent a large part of his cavalry against Parmenion on the left."],["The chariots charge","Darius launched his chariots at the troops under Alexander. Javelin-throwers stopped many, and the Macedonian ranks opened to let the rest pass harmlessly. Those that got through were then killed."],["Alexander's wedge strikes the Persian centre","Alexander drew his Companions out of the fight, formed a large wedge with other units and led it into the Persian centre, while the phalanx kept the Persian infantry engaged. By the widely held view, Darius then broke and fled."],["Parmenion is hard pressed and a gap opens","Parmenion's wing was apparently encircled and sent desperate messages, and Alexander turned back to help instead of pursuing Darius. A gap had opened in the phalanx, and Persian and Indian cavalry broke through towards the camp."],["The raiders are dispersed and the Persian right gives way","The rear reserve phalanx attacked and dispersed the raiders as they looted. Alexander fought the Persian right-wing cavalry and Mazaeus began to pull his forces back, and they fell into disorder under the Thessalian charge."]]],
  "Battle of Gettysburg": ["battle-of-gettysburg","e17d5c9c2c",[["Heth's brigades meet Buford's cavalry west of town","Buford's cavalry held the ridges west of the town to delay the Confederates. Two of Heth's brigades, part of Hill's corps, came east along the Chambersburg Pike and met his dismounted troopers."],["The I Corps arrives as the cavalry falls back","The Confederates pushed the Union cavalry east to McPherson Ridge. The vanguard of the I Corps then arrived and took up the fight beside it."],["Ewell's corps and the XI Corps come up from the north","Two divisions of Ewell's corps came down on the town from the north while the XI Corps hurried up. Rodes's and Early's divisions then assaulted and outflanked the I and XI Corps north and northwest of the town."],["The Union line falls back to Cemetery Hill","Howard ordered a retreat to Cemetery Hill, and the I Corps was driven back through the Seminary grounds and the streets of Gettysburg."],["The Union line forms a fishhook","Most of the Union infantry reached the field. The XII Corps stood on Culp's Hill, the remnants of the I and XI Corps on Cemetery Hill and the II Corps on Cemetery Ridge. Longstreet's corps came up to attack the Union left."],["Sickles's corps meets Longstreet's attack","Sickles had advanced the III Corps west to the Emmitsburg Road, away from Cemetery Ridge. Longstreet's divisions came forward and found it directly in their path."],["Pickett's Charge crosses the open ground","After a long bombardment, Pickett's division and brigades from Hill's corps stepped off the ridge and advanced to Cemetery Ridge. To the northeast Stuart's cavalry met Gregg's division and Custer's brigade, who stopped it reaching the rear of the Union infantry."]]],
  "Battle of Grunwald": ["battle-of-grunwald","a605980bbd",[["The two armies form opposing lines","The Polish-Lithuanian army stood with Polish heavy cavalry on its left, Lithuanian light cavalry on its right and mercenaries in the centre. The Order set its heavy cavalry against the Lithuanians and waited to be attacked."],["Vytautas attacks the Order's left flank","The Order had hoped the other side would attack first. Vytautas, supported by the Polish banners, went in against the Order's left flank, and the Lithuanian cavalry fought more than an hour."],["The Lithuanians retreat and the Poles are attacked","After more than an hour the Lithuanian light cavalry began a full retreat. Six of Wallenrode's banners did not pursue but joined Kuno's attack on the Polish side, and the second Polish line came up."],["The Grand Master's 16 banners and the last reserves","The royal banner of Kraków was lost and recaptured. Von Jungingen led 16 banners to the Polish side and the king committed his third line, and the fighting reached the Polish command."],["The Lithuanians return and strike from behind","The reorganised Lithuanians came back and attacked von Jungingen from the rear. The Order was by then outnumbered by the mass of Polish knights and the advancing Lithuanian cavalry."],["Von Jungingen is killed and the Order retreats","Von Jungingen tried to break through the Lithuanian lines and was killed. Surrounded and leaderless, the Order began to retreat, and part of the routed units made for their camp."],["The camp is broken","The knights tried to make a wagon fort of the camp, but the camp followers turned against them and joined the pursuit. The defence was soon broken and the camp ravaged; by one account more knights died there than on the field."]]],
  "Battle of Guandu": ["battle-of-guandu","482c6b8eb7",[["The armies face each other","Yuan Shao's army came to Yangwu, directly north of Guandu, and began building earthen fortifications. Cao Cao's troops held Guandu, where they had prepared fortifications."],["Yuan Shao advances into trench warfare","Yuan Shao's army moved slowly south from Yangwu and fought Cao Cao's men from behind earthen embankments that both sides made. Neither side overcame the other and the fight became a stalemate."],["Cavalry raid the grain depot at Gushi","Cao Cao sent small cavalry units under Xu Huang and Shi Huan against the depot at Gushi, where Han Meng guarded Yuan Shao's grain. Han Meng was routed and the grain carts were burnt."],["Supplies reach Wuchao and Cao Cao sets out","Chunyu Qiong brought an army of 10,000 with large food reserves to Wuchao. That night Cao Cao and Yue Jin took 5,000 infantry and cavalry from Guandu to attack it, leaving Cao Hong and Xun You in charge of the main camp."],["Wuchao burns while Yuan Shao strikes at Guandu","Cao Cao's raiders overran Chunyu Qiong's defences and set his forts on fire. Yuan Shao sent Zhang He and Gao Lan with his main army against the camp at Guandu and only a small cavalry unit to Wuchao."],["Zhang He and Gao Lan surrender; Cao Cao attacks","Zhang He and Gao Lan failed to break through at Guandu and surrendered to Cao Hong. Cao Cao's forces then launched a full attack on Yuan Shao's army."],["Yuan Shao flees north across the river","Yuan Shao's army was destroyed and much of its supplies were captured. Yuan Shao fled north across the Yellow River with about 800 cavalry, which was what was left of his army."]]],
  "Battle of Hastings": ["battle-of-hastings","75820f93d5",[["The two armies take their ground","The English formed a shield wall at the top of the slope. William drew up archers in front, spearmen behind them in three divisions, and kept his cavalry in reserve."],["The Normans attack uphill","The Norman archers shot uphill at the shield wall with little effect. The spearmen went forward and met a barrage of spears, axes and stones, and the cavalry came up in support without making headway."],["The Normans fall back and the English pursue","A general retreat began, blamed on the Breton division on William's left, and a rumour that William had been killed added to the confusion. Some of the English left the wall to pursue the fleeing invaders."],["William counter-attacks","William rode through his forces, showing his face and shouting that he was still alive. He led a counter-attack against the pursuing English, and the men who had left the wall were overwhelmed."],["The cavalry feign flight","After a probable lull William tried a new tactic: cavalry sent against the shield wall, then drawn back to pull the English into further pursuits. It was said to have been used twice."],["The assault on a thinned shield wall","The feigned flights did not break the line but probably thinned out the housecarls, who were replaced by men of the fyrd, and the shield wall held. Archers were used again before and during an assault by cavalry and infantry led by William."],["Harold dies and the English collapse","Harold died late in the battle, though the accounts of how he died contradict each other. His death left the English leaderless and they began to collapse, and the Normans pursued the fleeing troops."]]],
  "Battle of Hattin": ["battle-of-hattin","f9826b58f2",[["The Crusaders camp on the dry plateau","The Frankish army could not reach Tiberias by nightfall and turned towards the springs of Kafr Hattin. The Muslims placed themselves between the Franks and the water, so the Franks camped on the arid plateau, closely surrounded."],["Archers harass the Franks and the line advances","Gökböri's mounted archers shot at the Franks, who were blinded by smoke. Guy's brother Amalric formed the battle lines, and the army set out for the springs of Hattin, where Saladin's army blocked the way forward and any retreat."],["Raymond charges twice towards the lake","Count Raymond charged twice to break through to the water at Lake Tiberias. The second charge took him to the lake and he made his way to Tyre."],["The infantry flee to the Horns and Guy pitches tents","After Raymond escaped, most of the Christian infantry fled onto the Horns of Hattin and took no further part. Guy tried to pitch the tents again to block the Muslim cavalry, while the knights and mounted serjeants fought on."],["Three charges and the fall of Guy's tent","The Crusaders, surrounded, made three charges on Saladin's position. The Muslims rallied each time and drove them back to the hill, and then Guy's tent fell."]]],
  "Battle of Isandlwana": ["battle-of-isandlwana","126a9813e3",[["The Zulu army is found near the camp","Scouts chased some Zulus into a valley and saw most of the main force sitting in total quiet. Durnford had reached the camp with his men and a rocket battery, and Pulleine held the camp with the 24th and two guns."],["The Zulu advance and the British extend their line","Having been discovered, the Zulu leapt to the offensive in the horns and chest of the buffalo. Pulleine sent all six companies of the 24th out into an extended firing line, and Durnford's men moved forward."],["Durnford falls back to a donga","Durnford's men met part of the Zulu centre, retreated to a donga on the British right flank and formed a defensive line there. The rocket battery fell behind, fired three rockets and was overrun."],["Volleys pin the centre while the left horn swings out","For an hour or so the British volleys and the artillery held the Zulu centre. Meanwhile the left horn moved to outflank and envelop the British right."],["Durnford's withdrawal exposes the British flank","Durnford's men, who had fought the longest, began to withdraw. This exposed the right flank of the regulars, and G Company was overrun. Pulleine ordered the rest back to the camp, and they fell back fighting."],["The Zulu close in on the camp","The Zulu pressed into the camp and fought hand to hand. Evidence shows large groups of bodies around the camp, and the slaughter was complete in the area around it."]]],
  "Battle of Issus": ["battle-of-issus","1e7cfaa445",[["The two armies draw up on either side of the river","Alexander placed the Companions on his right and the Thessalian cavalry on the left of the phalanx under Parmenion. Darius set his heavy cavalry by the coast, the Greek mercenaries next, and the Cardaces along the river and into the foothills."],["The Persian cavalry charges across the river","The Persian horse crossed the river and charged Parmenion and the allied cavalry. The Thessalian cavalry faced a larger mass of heavy horse and charged and fell back by turns to buy time."],["The Macedonian centre is thrown back","The centre phalanx advanced across the river and up the fortified bank and suffered badly against the mercenaries. The Macedonians were forced to retreat across the river."],["The right wing breaks into the Persian line","Alexander led the hypaspists on foot against the Cardaces and punched a hole through the Persian line. The Agrianians drove back the skirmishers on his far right, which secured the flank of the Companions."],["Alexander's cavalry attacks Darius, who flees","Alexander mounted a horse at the head of the Companions and led a direct assault on Darius and his bodyguards. They fled from the battlefield."],["The Companions strike the mercenaries from behind","Alexander saw his left flank and centre in trouble and let Darius go. He crashed into the rear of the Greek mercenaries, who broke up and began to retreat."],["The Persians abandon their positions","The Persians saw that the Great King had gone and that the battle was being lost, and they fled in full rout. The Hellenic cavalry pursued them for as long as there was light."]]],
  "Battle of Iwo Jima": ["battle-of-iwo-jima","615aef1a1d",[["The Marines land on the southeastern coast","The 5th Marine Division landed in the southern sector and the 4th in the northern. The Japanese let the beach fill for just over an hour before they opened fire from their fortified positions."],["The 28th Marines cross the island; the 25th hit the Quarry","On the left the 28th Marines drove across the island at its narrowest point and cut off the Japanese on Mount Suribachi. On the right the 25th Marines attacked the Quarry in two prongs against heavy fire."],["Mount Suribachi is cut off and the flag goes up","The 28th Marines turned against the mountain. A patrol from E Company, 2nd Battalion, climbed the volcano and raised a flag at the top. Most of the Japanese there stayed in the tunnels and were generally killed."],["The 9th Marines attack at night towards Hill 362","In the north the fighting bogged down against positions the Japanese reoccupied through their tunnels. Erskine sent the 9th Marines forward in the dark, without a barrage, which helped take Hill 362."],["The Japanese counterattack for Hill 362","The local Japanese commander, against Kuribayashi's order, led a banzai charge of about 1,000 men to retake the hill. The Marines counted 784 Japanese dead the next day."],["The 3rd Marine Division reaches the northern coast","On the same day elements of the 3rd Marine Division reached the northern coast and split the Japanese defence in two. The plan stops drawing Inouye's force after its charge."],["The last Japanese counterattack","After the island was declared secure, a 300-man Japanese force attacked near Airfield No. 2. Army pilots, Seabees and Marines fought it for up to 90 minutes. The island was officially declared secure at 09:00 on 26 March."]]],
  "Battle of Jena–Auerstedt": ["battle-of-jena-auerstedt","763caf5e17",[["Gudin deploys at Hassenhausen","Gudin's Division was stopped outside Poppel by Prussian cavalry and artillery. Davout ordered it to deploy at Hassenhausen, while Schmettau's division and Blücher's cavalry formed to attack."],["Schmettau and Blücher push Gudin back","Schmettau's troops and Blücher's cavalry attacked together. They pushed Gudin's troops back to the village of Hassenhausen."],["Both sides bring up more troops","Wartensleben arrived with the Duke of Brunswick, who sent infantry to the Prussian left. The rest of the French cavalry came up on Gudin's left, and Friant's Division with the 12-pound artillery moved up on his right."],["Friant's squares advance and Hassenhausen is attacked","The advance of the French squares forced Blücher's cavalry back, and Blücher ordered his cavalry to attack. At that moment two of Wartensleben's regiments attacked Hassenhausen."],["The French cavalry strike from behind the hill","Davout hid his chasseurs à cheval behind a hill and released them at a crucial moment, putting Blücher's cavalry to flight. Brunswick then ordered a full assault on Hassenhausen."],["The French counter-attack","With Brunswick and Schmettau wounded, the Prussian command broke down. The Prince of Orange arrived and his command was split between the flanks, while Morand's Division secured Gudin's left. At 11 am Davout ordered his infantry to counter-attack."],["The Prussian centre breaks and the King orders a withdrawal","Schmettau's centre was broken and forced back over the Lissbach Stream. Blücher's cavalry was blown, Wartensleben was trying to reposition his troops, and the King ordered a withdrawal."]]],
  "Battle of Jutland": ["battle-of-jutland","45033f82a5",[["The fleets sail towards each other unseen","Beatty's ships ran east while Hipper's battlecruisers ran north, with Scheer's battle line following Hipper. Jellicoe's fleet was still out of sight to the north-west, and the Germans did not know it was at sea."],["The Run to the South","Hipper turned south-east to lead Beatty towards Scheer and opened fire. The British battlecruisers, with the 5th Battle Squadron behind them, followed on the south-western side. Hood was sent ahead from Jellicoe's fleet at about 16:05."],["Beatty turns north and the Germans follow","Beatty sighted the German battle line and turned his battlecruisers about to lead the Germans towards Jellicoe. The 5th Battle Squadron passed them going the other way and then turned north, drawing the fire of Hipper and of Scheer's leading battleships."],["Jellicoe deploys to the east","Jellicoe formed his six columns into a single battle line, deploying on the eastern column. Beatty's battlecruisers crossed the front of his line to join Hood, and Hood and Beatty engaged Hipper while Scheer's fleet came on from the south."],["Scheer's T is crossed and he turns away","The leading German battleships came out of the mist facing the whole British line, which crossed their T. Scheer ordered a turn together through 180°, and the German fleet disengaged under smoke and mist."],["Scheer turns back and turns away again","Scheer doubled back east, and by 19:15 Jellicoe had crossed his T again. Scheer turned west a second time and sent his battlecruisers and torpedo boats at the British to cover the turn."],["Scheer slips round the British rear in the night","Jellicoe headed south with a screen of cruisers and destroyers behind him to guard Scheer's expected escape route. Scheer instead crossed the British wake and made for Horns Reef, passing through the screen."]]],
  "Battle of Kadesh": ["battle-of-kadesh","65ee1a0596",[["The Hittites wait behind Kadesh","Muwatalli had placed his troops behind Old Kadesh. Ramesses had been told that the Hittite army was far off, so he was with the Amun division and his bodyguard while the Re division was still coming up to the camp."],["Hittite chariots cross the river and scatter the Re division","The Hittite chariots crossed the river and charged the middle of the Re division as it marched towards Ramesses. Caught in the open, the division scattered in all directions."],["The chariots attack the camp","The Hittite chariotry rounded to the north and attacked the Egyptian camp through the Amun shield wall, and panic spread among the Amun division. Some of the Re division fled north to the camp, pursued by Hittite chariots."],["Ramesses charges into the Hittite ranks","The momentum of the Hittite attack waned as chariots were impeded by obstacles in the camp. Ramesses defeated the first attackers and personally led several charges with his guard, some Amun chariots and survivors of the Re division."],["The Egyptian counter-attack drives them to the river","The Hittites, believing their enemies routed, stopped to plunder the camp. An Egyptian counter-attack then drove them back towards the Orontes and away from the camp, and in the pursuit lighter Egyptian chariots overtook Hittite chariots."],["A second Hittite counter-attack meets new arrivals","As the retreat reached the river, Muwatalli ordered another thousand chariots to counter-attack towards the camp. The Ne'arin from Amurru arrived and surprised them, and the Ptah division arrived from the south and threatened the Hittite rear."],["The Hittites are pinned against the Orontes","After six unsuccessful charges the Hittite forces were almost surrounded and the survivors were pinned against the Orontes. They abandoned their chariots and tried to swim the river, and many drowned. The Hittites held on to Kadesh."]]],
  "Battle of Karbala": ["battle-of-karbala","ecfee8d184",[["Both sides take up positions","After the morning prayer both sides formed up. Husayn put Zuhayr ibn al-Qayn on his right and Habib ibn Muzahir on his left, with the tents tied together and the ditch behind them. The Kufan wings stood opposite, with 500 horsemen on the route to the river."],["Volleys of arrows and duels","Zuhayr ibn al-Qayn tried to dissuade the Kufan soldiers from killing Husayn, but failed. The Kufans fired several volleys of arrows, and in the duels that followed several of Husayn's companions were slain."],["The Kufan right wing attacks and is repulsed","The right wing of the Kufans, under Amr ibn al-Hajjaj, attacked Husayn's force and was repulsed. Hand-to-hand fighting then paused and further volleys of arrows were exchanged."],["Shimr attacks and is repulsed","Shimr, who commanded the left wing of the Umayyad army, launched an attack. There were losses on both sides, and he was repulsed."],["Cavalry attacks and Ibn Sa'd brings in reinforcements","Cavalry attacks followed, and Husayn's cavalry resisted fiercely. Ibn Sa'd brought in armoured cavalry and five hundred archers."],["The tents are burned and Husayn's companions are encircled","Because the Kufans could approach only from the front, Ibn Sa'd ordered the tents burned, except the one Husayn and his family used. The flames hindered the Umayyad advance for a while. After noon prayers Husayn's companions were encircled."],["Husayn's relatives fight and Husayn is killed","Husayn's relatives, who had not fought until then, joined the battle, and among those slain was his son Ali al-Akbar. Shimr advanced with foot soldiers, the Kufans rushed at Husayn, and he was killed."]]],
  "Battle of Kohima": ["battle-of-kohima","7348993109",[["The garrison is penned in on Garrison Hill","The Japanese took GPT Ridge on the first day and pressed the garrison from the south and from the north, around the tennis court. The 161st Brigade, cut off at Jotsoma, could only support it with artillery."],["The Japanese cut the garrison in two","The Japanese took the area of the Deputy Commissioner's bungalow and Kuki Picquet, cutting the garrison in two. They did not go on to attack Garrison Hill, being exhausted by hunger and the fighting."],["The relief force breaks through","The 161st Brigade, with air, artillery and armour support, attacked towards Kohima from Jotsoma. After a day of heavy fighting its leading troops and tanks broke through and began to relieve the garrison."],["The 6th Brigade takes over Garrison Hill","The 6th Brigade replaced the original garrison, and Colonel Richards handed over command at 06:00 on 20 April. The Japanese went on trying to capture Garrison Hill for several more nights."],["The Japanese defend; two brigades try to outflank them","The Japanese held Kohima Ridge with the Left Force and Naga Village with the Centre Force. The 5th Brigade took a foothold in Naga Village and lost it to a counter-attack, while the 4th Brigade marched round Mount Pulebadze and took part of GPT Ridge by surprise."],["The ridge is cleared","Both outflanking moves failed, and the 2nd Division attacked along the ridge itself. Jail Hill and Kuki Picquet fell on 11 May. On 13 May a tank was dragged to the summit and crashed onto the tennis court, destroying the Japanese trenches there, and the ridge was cleared."],["Satō abandons Naga Village and the Japanese retreat","Satō left Naga Village and the positions north of the road on 31 May, which let XXXIII Corps outflank the Aradura Spur position. The 2nd Division then followed the retreat down the road and met troops from Imphal at Milestone 109 on 22 June."]]],
  "Battle of Kosovo": ["battle-of-kosovo","25794d8cee",[["The armies draw up on Kosovo Field","Lazar held the Serbian centre, Branković the right and Vuković the left, with heavy cavalry in front. Murad stood behind his Janissaries in the centre, Bayezid held the Ottoman right, Yakub the left, and archers stood on both wings."],["Ottoman archers shoot at the Serbian cavalry","The fighting began with Ottoman archers shooting at the Serbian cavalry, who then made ready to attack. The archers had been posted on the two wings of the Ottoman line."],["The Serbian cavalry breaks the Ottoman left","After forming a wedge, the Serbian cavalry charged and broke through the Ottoman left wing under Yakub. It was less successful against the centre and the right wing."],["Ottoman light troops counterattack","When the knights' charge was spent, light Ottoman cavalry and light infantry counterattacked, and the Serbian heavy armour became a disadvantage."],["The Serbian centre pushes back the Ottomans","In the centre the Serbian troops pushed the Ottomans back. Bayezid's wing barely held off the Bosnians under Vuković, who inflicted heavy losses on the Ottomans."],["Bayezid's counterattack routs the Serbian infantry","Bayezid led a counterattack that drove the Serbian forces back, and later in the day the Ottomans routed the Serbian infantry. Both flanks still held, and the Bosnians drifted toward the centre to cover the losses."],["Branković leaves and the rest yield the field","Branković saw no hope of victory and withdrew with as many men as he could. Some time afterwards the remaining Bosnian and Serbian forces gave up the field."]]],
  "Battle of Kursk": ["battle-of-kursk","fe27057016",[["The two armies take up their ground","The Germans planned two pincers on the Kursk salient, the 9th Army from the north and the 4th Panzer Army with Army Detachment Kempf from the south, while the 2nd Army held the west. The Central and Voronezh Fronts stood in fortified belts, with the Steppe Front in reserve."],["Both German pincers attack","The 9th Army attacked the Central Front in the north, and in the south the 4th Panzer Army and Army Detachment Kempf attacked across the Voronezh Front's sector. Each broke into the first belt of the Soviet defences."],["The northern attack is held at Ponyri and Olkhovatka","Model put the weight of the 9th Army on Ponyri and Olkhovatka, and the Central Front counterattacked and held both. By 10 July the Soviets had completely halted the German advance in the north."],["Soviet reserves arrive and the II SS turns to Prokhorovka","The 5th Guards Tank Army left the Steppe Front for Prokhorovka on 7 July. On 10 July Hoth turned the II SS Panzer Corps of the 4th Panzer Army north-east towards it, and on 11 July Army Detachment Kempf broke through."],["The tank battle at Prokhorovka","The 5th Guards Tank Army attacked down the slopes before Prokhorovka against the II SS Panzer Corps, which was attacking on its own plan. Neither side accomplished its objectives that day."],["Kutuzov opens in the north and Citadel ends in the south","The Bryansk and Western Fronts began Operation Kutuzov against the Orel salient, threatening the 9th Army's flank and rear, so that it went over fully to the defensive. In the south the II SS Panzer Corps was ordered on 17 July to end its offensive and begin to withdraw."],["Operation Rumyantsev drives south to Belgorod","The Voronezh and Steppe Fronts attacked the northern wing of Army Group South, aiming to destroy the 4th Panzer Army and Army Detachment Kempf. They drove through the German positions and by 5 August had liberated Belgorod."]]],
  "Battle of Königgrätz": ["battle-of-koniggratz","603bbea149",[["The armies take position either side of the river","The Prussians took their position west of the Bystřice river. The Austrian infantry stood partly fortified, with artillery behind it and cavalry in the rear, and the Saxons on the Austrian left."],["Austrian guns pin down the Prussian right","The Austrian artillery opened fire on the Prussian right under Herwarth von Bittenfeld. The Saxons fell back in good order and fired on the advancing Prussians from higher ground. Around 10 am the advance guard of seven battalions was pulled back to the river."],["The 7th Division advances into the Swiepwald","The Prussian centre went into the Swiepwald, where two Austrian corps met it. The First Army was ordered across the river to support the 7th Division, and Austrian artillery cut down the Prussians as they came out of the smoke."],["Pöckh's charge is raked by flanking fire","The Austrian centre moved to flank the 7th Division, and Pöckh's infantry charge forced it back to the outskirts of the forest. Flanking fire from the 8th Division then raked his battalion and killed its commander."],["The Second Army reaches the field","Crown Prince Frederick William arrived with the main bulk of the Second Army and hit the Austrian right flank as it retired from the Swiepwald. The 1st Guard came with it."],["The Austrian line breaks at Chlum and Probluz","The Second Army broke through the Austrian lines and took Chlum behind the centre, and the 1st Guard reached the Austrian artillery. On the other side the Army of the Elbe broke through the Austrian left and seized Probluz."],["Austrian cavalry cover the retreat","Benedek ordered a general retreat and a series of cavalry countercharges to back up his artillery and cover it. The cavalry kept the bridges over the Elbe open, at a cost of 2,000 men and almost as many horses."]]],
  "Battle of Lake Trasimene": ["battle-of-lake-trasimene","03faaf27d6",[["The Carthaginians lie hidden behind the hills","After dark Hannibal moved his army behind the hills and placed it on the reverse slopes: cavalry in the west, Gauls next, and the African and Iberian infantry in the east near the camp. The Romans, who could see the camp, set off along the lakeshore."],["The head of the column meets the easternmost Carthaginians","The Romans marched east in a thick morning mist, by the ancient accounts, with no cavalry scouts sent ahead. The leading men made contact with the most easterly Carthaginians and the signal to advance was given."],["The Carthaginians attack from the flank and rear","The whole Carthaginian force came down from the hills against the column's flank and rear. The Romans could not form even a rudimentary fighting line, and some fled while others gathered in groups to face the enemy on all sides."],["The trapped Romans fight on and are beaten down","Some Roman groups fought hard, especially in the centre, where the attacking Gauls suffered heavy casualties. After three hours the trapped Romans collapsed, and men who went into the lake were cut down or drowned."],["The 6,000 at the front push out through the defile","The trap did not close on the 6,000 Romans at the head of the column. They pushed out through the defile against little opposition and marched on."],["The 6,000 are surrounded and surrender","Later in the day pursuing Carthaginians surrounded the 6,000 Romans. They surrendered to a force under Maharbal on the promise of being disarmed and freed."]]],
  "Battle of Leipzig": ["battle-of-leipzig","2a5172b5b7",[["The French round Leipzig, the Coalition armies closing","Napoleon drew his army round Leipzig, with Ney and Marmont on the north, MacDonald on the east, Poniatowski and Augereau on the south-east and Bertrand's corps at Lindenau. The Coalition armies stood round them."],["The Coalition attacks from all sides","The Allies attacked from every side and completely encircled the French army. After over nine hours of fighting the French were slowly forced back towards Leipzig."],["Bertrand drives the Austrians from Lindenau","On the western side the French IV Corps drove the Austrian III Corps away from Lindenau. This broke the encirclement and cleared the way for the French retreat."],["The French withdraw in the night","The French army was ordered to withdraw silently through Leipzig and over its single bridge across the Elster. A rearguard was left to hold the city for a day or a little longer, while the Allies did not realise that the army was leaving."],["The Allies storm Leipzig","The Allies learned of the evacuation at 7 am and attacked from the north, south and east. Oudinot's rearguard held them up in street fighting among barricades and houses."],["The bridge is blown and the rearguard is cut off","A corporal fired the fuses of the only bridge over the Elster while it was crowded with French troops and Oudinot's rearguard was still in Leipzig. The panic became a rout in which thousands died and 30,000 were captured. The rest of the army had crossed."]]],
  "Battle of Lepanto": ["battle-of-lepanto","d158e68fb6",[["The two fleets draw up in line","The Holy League fleet formed four divisions in a north-south line, its left towards the coast. The Ottoman fleet faced it with a right, a centre and a left under Uluç Ali offshore in the south."],["The galleasses open fire","The wind shifted to favour the Christians shortly before contact. Galleasses in front of their line fired at close quarters at the foremost Turkish galleys and confused their battle array."],["Barbarigo and Sirocco meet by the shore","First contact was between Barbarigo and Sirocco near the northern shore. Barbarigo tried to keep close to the coast, but Sirocco inserted galleys between his line and the shore. Barbarigo was killed, Bazán's reserve arrived and Sirocco was killed."],["The centres clash around the flagships","The centres met so hard that Ali Pasha's galley drove into the Real as far as the fourth rowing bench. Spanish Tercio infantry and Turkish janissaries fought hand to hand around the two flagships."],["The Turkish flagship is taken","Colonna came alongside the Real and mounted a counter-attack. The Turkish flagship was boarded and swept, and Ali Pasha was killed. After two hours of fighting the Turks were beaten on the left and in the centre."],["Doria sails south and Uluç Ali turns on the centre","Doria kept sailing south instead of taking his station and opened a wide gap with the centre. Uluç Ali swung round and fell on Colonna's southern flank, attacking some fifteen galleys round the flagship of the Knights of Malta."],["Bazán's reserve arrives and Uluç Ali retreats","The arrival of the reserve under Bazán kept Uluç Ali from breaking into the Christian centre. He was forced to retreat and escaped the battle with the captured flag of the Knights of Malta."]]],
  "Battle of Leuctra": ["battle-of-leuctra","c19f8c4702",[["The armies draw up","The Spartans stood with their own troops on the right and their allies on the left. Epaminondas set his Thebans in a very deep column on the left, opposite the Spartan right, with the other Boeotians further right and back."],["The cavalry meet and the Spartan horse is beaten","The battle opened with a cavalry engagement between the lines. The Theban cavalry, the more experienced of the two, quickly defeated the Spartan cavalry."],["The Spartan cavalry flees and the Thebans charge","The Spartan cavalry fled back through its own infantry and disordered the Spartan line just as the Thebans charged. The other Boeotians held back, by one account withdrawing gradually as they were attacked."],["The Spartan right holds, then is pushed back","The Spartan line held long enough for the mortally wounded Cleombrotus to be carried off alive. It then fell back under the pressure of the Theban mass."],["The Spartan wing is routed and the left gives way","The Spartan right wing was pushed back, turned, routed and fled. The Spartan left wing gave way when its men saw the right being pushed back."]]],
  "Battle of Madagascar": ["battle-of-madagascar","00ff9c5186",[["The landings west of Diego-Suarez","The first wave of the 29th Brigade landed at Ambararata Bay and the 17th Brigade at Courrier Bay. The Vichy troops were concentrated around Diego-Suarez, and the defences of Antisarane lay ahead."],["Both brigades advance inland","The 17th Brigade took the town of Diego-Suarez after crossing mangrove swamp and bush. The 29th Brigade, with the tanks, advanced 21 miles towards Antisarane against light resistance."],["A frontal assault on the line fails","The 29th Brigade and its tanks attacked the defences head on. Ten of the twelve tanks were destroyed by the end of the day, and the British had not known how strong the line was."],["The South Lancashires work round the line","The South Lancashires went round the Vichy defences through the swamps and rough ground, and swung behind the line. They fired on the defences from behind and took a radio station and a barracks."],["Marines land in the rear and the line is broken","HMS Anthony ran past the harbour defences and landed fifty Royal Marines among the Vichy rear. The 17th Brigade broke through the defences. The South Lancashires withdrew after their radio failed."],["Antisarane surrenders","With the Vichy defence broken, Antisarane surrendered that evening, though substantial Vichy forces withdrew to the south."]]],
  "Battle of Magnesia": ["battle-of-magnesia","74cd5273d4",[["The armies line up","The two armies lined up facing each other without fighting. The Romans stood with their left towards the rivers, and Antiochus took the Roman challenge on the third day after the last Roman advance."],["Eumenes drives off the chariots","Eumenes sent forward archers, slingers and spearmen against the scythed chariots. The chariots suffered heavy casualties and fled in panic, throwing the camel archers and cataphracts behind them into confusion."],["The Seleucid left is broken","Eumenes charged with his cavalry before the cataphracts could reorganise. The Roman and Pergamene cavalry broke through the Seleucid left flank, and the cataphracts fled to the Seleucid camp."],["The infantry beside the phalanx give way","The Galatians, Cappadocians and mercenary infantry were attacked at once by the Roman centre and right. They retreated, which left the left flank of the phalanx exposed."],["Antiochus attacks on the right","Antiochus led his cataphracts and agema cavalry against the Latin infantry while the argyraspides engaged the legionaries. The Roman infantry broke ranks and retreated to their camp, where the Thracians and Macedonians reinforced them."],["The phalanx breaks","The phalanx held against the Roman infantry but could not drive off the archers and slingers who shot at it. It began a slow retreat, the elephants between its units panicked, and the phalangites threw down their weapons and left the field."],["Antiochus withdraws","Antiochus's cavalry came back to reinforce the centre after his army had already dispersed. He gathered the surviving troops and retreated to Sardes."]]],
  "Battle of Manzikert": ["battle-of-manzikert","205619c668",[["The Byzantines form up and march out","The Byzantine army gathered into battle formation and marched on the Seljuk positions, with Bryennios on the left, Theodore Alyates on the right and the emperor in the centre. Andronikos Doukas held the reserve in the rear, and the Seljuks stood in a crescent about four kilometres away."],["Horse archers draw the crescent round them","Seljuk horse archers shot at the Byzantines as they drew closer. The centre of the crescent kept falling back while the wings moved round to surround the advancing troops."],["The Byzantines take the camp, but the wings fray","The Byzantine centre pressed on despite the arrows and captured Alp Arslan's camp by the end of the afternoon. The wings, where the arrows did most damage, almost broke up as units tried to force a pitched battle, and the Seljuk cavalry simply disengaged when challenged."],["The withdrawal is ordered and misunderstood","With the Seljuks avoiding battle, Romanos ordered a withdrawal. The right wing misunderstood the order, and Doukas ignored it and marched his reserve straight back to the camp outside Manzikert instead of covering the retreat."],["The Seljuks attack and the right wing breaks","With the Byzantines confused, the Seljuks attacked. The right wing was almost at once routed, its men thinking they had been betrayed."],["The left wing is routed and the centre encircled","The left wing under Bryennios held out a little longer but was soon routed. The Seljuks encircled what remained of the centre, including the emperor and the Varangian Guard, and Romanos was wounded and taken prisoner."]]],
  "Battle of Marathon": ["battle-of-marathon","eb54fe68af",[["The two armies form their lines","The two tribes in the Greek centre were drawn up four ranks deep and the tribes on their flanks eight deep. One explanation is that this made the Athenian line as long as the Persian line."],["The Greeks advance and the lines meet","On a signal from Miltiades the Athenians advanced and probably broke into a run at the limit of the archers' effectiveness. They went through the Persian arrows, protected for the most part by their armour, and met the enemy line."],["The Greek centre is pushed back","The fighting lasted a long time. In the centre the Persians and Sacae prevailed and broke through in pursuit inland, while on each wing the Athenians and Plataeans prevailed."],["The Persian wings collapse and the Greek wings turn in","The Athenian wings quickly routed the Persian levies on the flanks. They then turned inwards to surround the Persian centre, which had been more successful against the thin Greek centre."],["The Persian centre breaks and flees to the ships","The Persian centre broke in panic towards its ships, pursued by the Greeks. Some, unaware of the local terrain, ran towards the swamps, where unknown numbers drowned."]]],
  "Battle of Marengo": ["battle-of-marengo","4847f882cc",[["The two armies take their ground","The Austrians crossed the Bormida in three bodies. Victor held Marengo and the Fontanone with Kellermann's cavalry on his left, Lannes stood to the north-east, and Monnier and the Guard waited east of Castel Ceriolo."],["The Austrian assaults at the Fontanone fail","Melas sent his divisions at Marengo across the stream one after another. Forced into a funnel by the ground, the first attack failed under fire from two sides, and the second was beaten off by 11 am."],["Ott takes Castel Ceriolo and presses the French right","Ott's leading troops took Castel Ceriolo and began to press the French right flank. Monnier's division and the Guard were brought across to extend and shore up that flank."],["Victor withdraws from Marengo","The Austrians bridged the Fontanone and took Marengo. Victor could no longer hold his positions and fell back south-east to the vine belt, with Lannes mirroring the move, while O'Reilly took Stortiglione."],["The French fall back and the Guard is destroyed","Ott defeated Monnier, and two thirds of his division retreated north-east. The Guard, sent to delay Schellenberg's column, was surprised and destroyed. The French fell back about 3 km towards San Giuliano as the Austrian centre formed a pursuit column."],["Desaix's force arrives and meets the column","Boudet's division was deployed in front of San Giuliano and advanced in line of brigades against the head of the Austrian column, defeating its leading brigade. The column came on along the road."],["Kellermann's charge breaks the Austrian centre","Kellermann's heavy cavalry charged the left flank of the Austrian grenadier brigade, which disintegrated. The Austrian centre lost heart and rushed to the rear while the French infantry line advanced westward."]]],
  "Battle of Marignano": ["battle-of-marignano","5ae6dcf52e",[["The French form up and the Swiss approach","The French, who had not expected battle, formed in three divisions. The vanguard held two shallow rises with crossbowmen, a Landsknecht square and arquebusiers, the king's force stood two kilometres behind, and the Swiss came on in three divisions."],["The Swiss forlorn hope charges the guns","A forlorn hope detached from the Swiss vanguard and charged the grand battery with lowered pikes. It drove back the Landsknecht defenders and captured a few of the guns, the speed of its advance making the French fire ineffective."],["Bourbon's cavalry strikes the flank","Cavalry from the French right counter-attacked the forlorn hope's flank and drove it back to the shelter of the Swiss vanguard."],["Night fighting by moonlight","Smoke and darkness hid the fighting. French cavalry charges, often led by the king, threw back temporary Swiss gains, and the pursuing horse of the first counter-attack fell back a full kilometre after heavy losses."],["The Swiss charge the guns again at dawn","The Swiss reformed their largest phalanx and charged the reassembled battery. Point-blank cannon fire slowed it and stopped it pushing farther, though the Landsknechts were again driven back, and a cavalry charge led by Bayard forced the Swiss to give ground."],["The Swiss turn on the French left","Baffled by the artillery, the Swiss turned their assault on Alençon's left-wing division. They made some headway, and the attack was then thrown back."],["The Venetians arrive and the Swiss withdraw","The Venetian forces under d'Alviano arrived and turned the tide. With their attacks repulsed everywhere, the Swiss gave ground and withdrew in a single large open square."]]],
  "Battle of Midway": ["battle-of-midway","f117984887",[["The fleets take their places","Nimitz sent Fletcher's force to the Midway area before the Japanese arrived, and the two American task forces waited northeast of the atoll. Nagumo's carriers approached from the west, with Yamamoto's ships several hundred miles behind them."],["The Japanese strike Midway, and Midway's bombers strike back","Nagumo launched his first attack on Midway at 04:30. Bombers that had taken off from the atoll before the Japanese arrived attacked his carriers. The Japanese repelled them, and the strike on Midway left its defences largely intact."],["American torpedo squadrons are shot down","The American carriers had launched from 07:00, and their torpedo squadrons reached the Japanese carriers without fighter escort. Most were shot down and none of their torpedoes hit, but the attacks drew the Japanese fighters out of position."],["Dive bombers hit three of the four carriers","Dive bombers from Enterprise and Yorktown arrived while the Japanese fighters were drawn off. Kaga and Akagi were hit and set on fire, and Sōryū was hit too. All three were eventually abandoned and scuttled."],["Hiryū strikes Yorktown","Hiryū, the one carrier still operating, sent two waves after the American aircraft. Three bombs hit Yorktown in the first, and two torpedoes crippled her in the second. Fletcher moved his command staff to the cruiser Astoria."],["Enterprise's bombers cripple Hiryū","A Yorktown scout found Hiryū, and Enterprise launched 24 dive bombers. Four bombs, possibly five, hit her and left her ablaze. She stayed afloat for several hours and sank after being spotted the next morning."],["Both sides draw back","Spruance withdrew to the east during the night, fearing a night encounter with Japanese surface ships. Yamamoto had detached a cruiser force to bombard Midway, then ordered a general withdrawal to the west, and the cruisers turned back."]]],
  "Battle of Mikatagahara": ["battle-of-mikatagahara","65a5936431",[["The armies take their ground on the plain","Ieyasu drew up his forces in a line on the plain of Mikatagahara. Shingen arrayed his men in the fish-scale formation in four lines, with the vanguard first and Baba Nobuharu in the fourth."],["The Takeda cavalry overruns the Tokugawa right","As snow began to fall the Tokugawa arquebusiers opened fire. Naitō Masatoyo's vanguard cavalry attacked all the same and overran Honda Tadakatsu's segment on the Tokugawa right."],["The Oda force is routed and the rear is charged","The Takeda horsemen turned on the Oda reinforcements, who were quickly overrun and routed, and then charged the Tokugawa rear."],["A second Takeda attack breaks the line","Shingen withdrew his vanguard to rest and sent fresh horsemen under Katsuyori in a two-pronged charge, closely followed by the main body. Their weight drove the Tokugawa army into a disorderly retreat."],["Ieyasu flees and his units are overrun","Ieyasu left the field, and several of his retainers died as their units were encircled and overrun. The Takeda vanguard under Baba Nobuharu and Yamagata Masakage came on and stopped short of Hamamatsu to make camp for the night."],["A night raid and the Takeda withdrawal","A small band of Tokugawa foot soldiers and gunners attacked the Takeda camp and threw the vanguard into confusion. Shingen, uncertain of the Tokugawa strength, decided to withdraw to his own territories."]]],
  "Battle of Mohács": ["battle-of-mohacs","6220e7073b",[["The two armies take up their positions","The Hungarians stood in two lines on a waterlogged plain, mercenary infantry and artillery in the centre of the first with heavy cavalry on both flanks, and levy infantry and reserve cavalry behind. The Ottomans faced them with Ibrahim Pasha in the centre."],["The Hungarian cavalry charges the Ottoman front","In the early afternoon Archbishop Pál Tomori led a charge that drove back the Ottoman front line and caused disorder among the irregulars. The Ottoman centre gave ground."],["The Ottoman centre falls back and the reserves follow","Ibrahim Pasha's centre fell back in a controlled manoeuvre and formed a crescent that exposed the flanks of the advancing cavalry. King Louis and the reserves moved in support, and the Hungarian centre advanced as well."],["The Ottoman wings counterattack and the guns open fire","The Ottomans struck with cavalry and light infantry from both wings, while Janissaries and artillery fired from prepared positions. The Hungarian army had advanced too far and too fast."],["The Hungarian army is encircled and its lines collapse","Surrounded and under concentrated musket and cannon fire, the Hungarian army collapsed within two hours. Over 14,000 Hungarian troops died by one estimate."]]],
  "Battle of Mycale": ["battle-of-mycale","afff0bd753",[["The Greeks land and form two wings","The Greeks landed out of sight of the Persian camp and formed two wings. The left stood from the shore to the foothills of the mountain, the right on the hills. The Persians had a palisaded camp with the fleet beached behind it."],["The Greek left meets the Persians alone","The Persians came out of their camp and set up their shield wall. The Greek left began fighting while the right was still crossing the hills."],["The right comes up and breaks the shield wall","The other Greek wing, under heavy arrow fire, chose to fight at close quarters and broke the wall by shoving. Tigranes and Mardontes died in the fighting."],["The Persians flee to their camp","The Persians broke their lines and escaped to their camp. The Greek soldiers followed them in, and most of the Persian troops fled from the camp. The ethnic Persians grouped together and fought those who entered."],["The left outflanks the camp","The Greek left arrived, outflanked the camp and fell on the rear of the Persians who remained, which completed the rout."],["The Milesians turn on the Persians","The Milesians guarding the passes first misdirected the fleeing Persians, who ended up back among the Greek troops, and then began killing them."],["The fleet and fortifications are burned","The Greeks burned the Persian fleet after a heavily contested fight with the Persian marines, once the loot had been taken from the ships and placed on the beach. The fortifications were burnt too."]]],
  "Battle of Myriokephalon": ["battle-of-myriokephalon","8bbf2c8ac4",[["The Byzantine column enters the pass","The Byzantine divisions entered the pass in a long column, vanguard first and the rear division last. The Seljuks held the heights above it, perhaps not yet fully deployed."],["The leading divisions clear the pass","The vanguard and main division went through with few casualties. Their infantry climbed the slopes and forced the Seljuks back to higher ground, while the divisions behind did not take this precaution."],["The trap closes on the right wing and baggage","With the first two divisions out of the pass and the rear about to enter, the Turks attacked from the heights. The right wing broke and Baldwin was killed. The Turks then shot down the draught animals of the baggage and siege trains and choked the road."],["The left wing is struck and a dust-storm falls","The left wing also took heavy casualties and John Kantakouzenos was killed. The remaining Byzantine troops panicked as the Turks began to attack the rear, a dust-storm came down, and Manuel sat down to await his fate."],["Manuel forms up and leaves the pass","Roused by his officers, Manuel restored discipline and formed a defensive order. His troops pushed past the wrecked baggage and out of the pass to the van and main divisions, who had built a fortified camp. The rear division arrived later with few casualties."],["The camp is attacked and counterattacks follow","The Seljuk mounted archers attacked the camp through the night and were repulsed. Next day the Turks circled the camp shooting arrows, and Manuel ordered two counterattacks, but there was no renewal of a general action."]]],
  "Battle of Nagashino": ["battle-of-nagashino","6ce3b1b396",[["The allies camp at Shitaragahara","Nobunaga halted at Shitaragahara without going to the relief of Nagashino Castle and camped west of it, Nobutada on Mount Shinmido and Ieyasu on Mount Takamatsu facing the river. The Takeda were still around the castle."],["Takeda marches east of Shitaragahara","Katsuyori led his main force towards Shitaragahara and left 3,000 men at the siege. The Oda and Tokugawa forces advanced and the two sides faced each other across the Rengo River. Sakai set out at night with a flying column."],["Sakai attacks the fort from behind","Sakai's column fell on the Takeda soldiers defending the fort from behind Mount Tobigasu. The soldiers in the fort were defeated, and the enemy now stood behind Katsuyori's main army."],["The column joins the castle and drives off the besiegers","The detachment entered the castle in cooperation with its own soldiers and drove the Takeda forces off. Katsuyori's main army was left with its retreat cut off and the enemy behind it."],["The Takeda charge the allied camps","With nowhere to go, the Takeda main force attacked the Tokugawa and Oda camps in front of it, one after the other."],["Matchlocks and fences stop the charges","Nobunaga came to Mount Takamatsu, took command of the whole army and sent a matchlock corps to the front line. The Takeda charged repeatedly, and each time guns and horse defence fences stopped them and they fell back."],["The Takeda army routs","By midday the result was clear and the Takeda forces began their rout. Nobunaga pounced on them as their ranks gave way and inflicted more casualties."]]],
  "Battle of Navarino": ["battle-of-navarino","e24607c73c",[["The Allied squadrons enter the bay","The Ottoman and Egyptian fleet lay anchored in a horseshoe of three lines. The Allied ships came through the southern entrance in two lines, British and French to starboard and the Russians to port, slightly behind."],["The Allies anchor in the crescent","The three British ships of the line anchored facing the Ottoman centre. The French took the side facing the Ottoman left wing, and the Russians the right end of the crescent. Allied captains had orders to fire only if attacked."],["Fighting breaks out at the entrance","Dartmouth's boats were fired on when they tried to stop an Ottoman fireship, and the shooting spread along the line. Breslaw left the French formation to reinforce Albion and Azov in the centre."],["The centre fights at anchor","Ships fired at very close range, with little room to manoeuvre. The French silenced the fort's battery. Asia concentrated her fire on Fahti Bahri, and Breslaw moved to the junction of the British and Russian lines."],["The Ottoman front line is despatched","All three Ottoman ships of the line and most of the large frigates of the first line had been despatched. The small British and French vessels stopped the fireships, and only the first attack on Scipion got home."],["The smaller vessels are destroyed","The Allied ships of the line, all still operational, fired on the second and third lines. Codrington tried twice to order a ceasefire, but his signals were hidden by smoke or ignored. Of 78 vessels, just eight stayed seaworthy."]]],
  "Battle of Nicopolis": ["battle-of-nicopolis","b952ff08a7",[["The armies take position","The French knights rode out with their backs to Nicopolis. The Hungarians, Hospitallers and Germans stayed behind them, while the Turks stood on the slope to the south with their reserve of sipahis out of sight on the plateau."],["The French charge the Turkish front line","The French knights charged and crushed the untrained conscripts at the front of the Turkish army. The conscripts broke and the knights pressed on towards the trained infantry behind them."],["The knights fight through the stakes","The knights came under heavy fire from archers and were held up by rows of sharpened stakes. Many were unhorsed or dismounted to pull the stakes up, and the Turkish infantry fled behind the sipahis."],["The sipahis counter-attack on the plateau","The French knights reached the plateau expecting to find the Turks in flight and met the sipahis whom Bayezid had kept in reserve. Some knights fled down the slope, and the rest fought on as sipahis also swept down along the flanks."],["Nevers is taken and the French yield","The Turks threatened to overwhelm Nevers, and his bodyguard threw themselves to the ground to plead for his life. The Turks took Nevers prisoner, and seeing this, the rest of the French yielded."],["The Wallachians leave and the Serbs arrive","The Hungarians and their allies fought in confused combat on the plain, while the Transylvanians and Wallachians decided the day was lost and left the field. A reinforcement of Serbian knights then came to the Turks."],["Sigismund's force is overwhelmed","Sigismund, the Master of the Hospitallers and the Germans fought to prevent the envelopment, with heavy losses on both sides. The force was overwhelmed; Sigismund and the Master escaped by boat, and the rest of his army surrendered."]]],
  "Battle of Okinawa": ["battle-of-okinawa","dd6608b696",[["The Kerama Islands are taken","Soldiers of the 77th Infantry Division landed in the Kerama Islands, 15 miles west of Okinawa. Task Force 58 lay to the east of the island, the Thirty-Second Army held the south and Udo Force the north."],["The main landing at the Hagushi beaches","The XXIV Corps and the III Amphibious Corps landed on the Hagushi beaches on the west coast. Off the Minatoga beaches in the south-east the 2nd Marine Division made a demonstration to deceive the Japanese."],["The Marines close the north and the 77th takes Ie Shima","The 6th Marine Division sealed off the Motobu Peninsula, where Udo Force was cornered around Mount Yaedake, and cleared it on 18 April. The 77th Division assaulted Ie Shima on 16 April."],["The advance south meets Kakazu Ridge","The 96th and 7th Infantry Divisions wheeled south and met Japanese soldiers in fortified caves at Kakazu Ridge, part of Shuri's outer defences. The advance went on, with heavy casualties on both sides."],["Ushijima attacks across the front","Ushijima, influenced by General Chō, took the offensive and the Thirty-Second Army attacked American positions across the whole front on the evening of 12 April. The assaults of the following nights were repulsed."],["Shuri is left and the army withdraws south","The 6th Marine Division occupied the ruins of Naha, and Marines of the 1st Marine Division took Shuri Castle on 29 May, which the Thirty-Second Army had left. The army moved nearly 30,000 men to the Kiyan Peninsula."],["The last pocket in the south","Elements of the 6th Marine Division landed on the Oroku Peninsula, and the naval troops died by suicide in their tunnels on 13 June. By 17 June the rest of the Thirty-Second Army was in a small pocket in the far south."]]],
  "Battle of Oriskany": ["battle-of-oriskany","ffc1378807",[["The column enters the ravine","Herkimer's column marched towards Fort Stanwix and went down into the ravine and over the stream. The Haudenosaunee lay hidden on both sides of it, and the Royal Yorkers waited behind a nearby rise."],["The warriors open fire too early","The Haudenosaunee fired before the plan called for it, and the Patriots were taken by surprise. Colonel Ebenezer Cox was shot from his horse in the first volley, and Herkimer's leg was shattered."],["The rear of the column flees","The trap had been sprung too early and part of the column had not yet entered the ravine. Most of these men panicked and fled, and some of the warriors pursued them, leaving dead and wounded for several miles."],["The militia rally on the crest","About half of Herkimer's men were still fighting 30 minutes in. They rallied and fought out of the ravine to the crest on its west side, and Herkimer regrouped them on higher ground during a one-hour thunderstorm."],["The Royal Yorkers advance in turned coats","When the fighting resumed, the Royal Yorkers advanced with their coats turned inside out, to pass as a relief party from the fort. The ruse failed when a Patriot captain recognised a Loyalist neighbour, and close combat followed."],["Willett's sortie raids the camps","Herkimer's messengers reached the fort at around 11 am. Lieutenant Colonel Marinus Willett led 250 men out to raid the nearly deserted camps to the south, driving off the few people left in them."],["The warriors leave to protect their camps","A guard ran to the field to warn the warriors, who broke off with the Seneca signal to retire and headed for the camps. This forced the smaller number of German and Loyalist fighters to withdraw as well."]]],
  "Battle of Plassey": ["battle-of-plassey","6dbb1d41c2",[["The two armies take their ground","The Nawab's army formed an arc with the French guns at the larger tank in front. Clive lined up the Europeans in the centre with a sepoy division on each side, and posted four guns at the kilns to answer the French."],["The guns open and Clive withdraws to the grove","The French guns fired the first shot and the rest of the Nawab's artillery followed. The British lost 10 Europeans and 20 sepoys by 8:30, and Clive left the advanced guns at the kilns and drew the army back into the grove."],["Rain, and Mir Madan Khan's charge is driven back","A rainstorm drenched the Nawab's gunpowder, while the British covered theirs. Mir Madan Khan led his cavalry in a charge, but British grapeshot mortally wounded him and drove his men back."],["The Nawab's army turns back to its entrenchments","The Nawab's army stopped its cannonade and began turning back north to its entrenchments. This left St. Frais and his artillery without support."],["Kilpatrick advances and the British take the larger tank","Major Kilpatrick advanced on his own with two companies and two field pieces towards the French position. Clive brought up the rest of the army behind him, and the French artillery retreated to the redoubt."],["Mir Jafar's division turns towards the grove and is halted","The Nawab's left arm, which had lingered behind the rest, turned and marched towards the grove. Clive took it for a threat to his baggage and sent three platoons and a field piece, whose fire halted it."],["The British take the hill and the redoubt","Clive ordered a three-pronged attack on the redoubt and the hill east of it. Major Coote's grenadiers took the hill at 16:30, the redoubt fell after St. Frais retreated, and by 17:00 the British held the entrenchment and the camp."]]],
  "Battle of Plataea": ["battle-of-plataea","3669b2b27d",[["The two lines face each other across the Asopos","The Persians lined up north of the river with their left wing facing the Spartans and Tegeans. Next to them stood the Medes and their neighbours, then the Boeotians and other Greek allies of Persia facing the Athenians."],["The night retreat goes wrong","The Greek centre moved off late at night and went too far, ending at the Temple of Hera. The Spartans and Tegeans had not begun to move by daybreak, and a lochos under Amompharetus was left behind. The Athenians were blocked by the scattered centre."],["Mardonius hurries after the Greeks","Mardonius learned of the retreat and rushed to Pausanias's position with the Persian left wing. The Persian cavalry attacked the Spartans and Tegeans, who had reached the Temple of Demeter, and the Athenians were engaged by the Boeotians."],["The Tegeans and Spartans charge","The Tegeans ran at the Persian line as arrows fell, and once Pausanias had favourable omens the Spartans charged too. Artabazos brought up 40,000 men in a tight formation, slowly. Amompharetus's men had joined the Spartans."],["Mardonius is killed and the Persian line breaks","Aeimnestus killed Mardonius with a stone. His guards broke and retreated and the rest of the army followed. The Athenians still fought the Thebans until they too retreated. Artabazos led his men away to Phocis, avoiding the camp."],["The Greeks follow to the Persian camp","The Spartans reached the camp first and waited for the Athenians, who knew how to scale walls. The Tegeans were the first to breach them. The Persians could not form up and took heavy losses."]]],
  "Battle of Poitiers (1356)": ["battle-of-poitiers-1356","9382b430ce",[["The two armies take their ground","The Anglo-Gascons held a wooded hill, the Prince's division behind the other two as a reserve. The French drew up in four divisions, the first about 500 yards from the English."],["The French cavalry charge the two wings","Audrehem's horsemen rode at Warwick's division and Clermont's at Salisbury's. On the left the marsh kept them from the archers. On the right they reached the gap in the hedge and a fierce melee began."],["The first division is beaten and the second attacks","The cavalry were driven back with heavy losses and the first division was forced to withdraw under the English archers' fire. The second division, 4,000 strong, then attacked at the hedge, and the Prince committed his reserve."],["The Dauphin's division withdraws, and Orléans leaves","After two hours the Dauphin's division withdrew in order. Orléans marched away with half of the third division. The rest made a feeble attack that was easily repelled, and some of Warwick's men left their line in pursuit."],["The King's division advances; the Captal rides round","The King's division, swelled by survivors of the earlier attacks, marched across the gap with the Oriflamme unfurled. The Anglo-Gascons sent a small mounted force under the Captal de Buch round the French flank."],["The Prince orders a general advance","The Black Prince ordered a general advance and the Anglo-Gascons moved out of their position. Warwick's men returning from the pursuit reinforced the line as the Captal's force worked round to the French rear."],["The French are attacked from behind and break","The Captal's 160 men struck the French rear. The division fell apart, and those still fighting around the King were forced into a loop of the River Miosson. They were surrounded, and John and his youngest son surrendered."]]],
  "Battle of Poltava": ["battle-of-poltava","81fdad3430",[["The Swedes form south of the redoubts","The Swedish columns formed in the night 600 metres south of the redoubts, with the Russian cavalry behind the redoubts and the camp beyond. The infantry was in place early, but the cavalry arrived late."],["The Swedish wings pass the redoubts","Roos' column overran the first two redoubts but stalled at the third. Lewenhaupt's battalions on the right and Hamilton's cavalry on the left went past the redoubts, and the Russian cavalry retreated."],["Lewenhaupt withdraws west and Roos is cut off","Rehnskiöld stopped the pursuit and ordered Lewenhaupt to withdraw to the west, where the wings waited for Roos' battalions. Roos led his remaining 1,500 men into the Yakovetski woods to the east."],["Roos is driven back and surrenders","The Russians retook the first two redoubts and attacked Roos with ten regiments. He fell back towards Poltava, took refuge in an abandoned fort and surrendered at 9:30 a.m."],["Peter advances and the Swedish line moves forward","Peter led his infantry out of the camp, with dragoons on his right and Menshikov's cavalry on his left. Rehnskiöld ordered Lewenhaupt and the Swedish line forward, and the Russian first line retreated towards its second line."],["The Swedish left flank breaks","The Russian line was longer, and the Swedish infantry on the left flank threw down their weapons and fled. A gap opened in the Swedish line, the Russians filled it, and Menshikov's cavalry attacked the Swedes from the rear."],["The Swedish assault disintegrates","Small groups of Swedes broke through and escaped to the south through the Budyschenski woods, while many of the rest were overwhelmed, ridden down or captured. Charles ordered a retreat to the woods with what forces he could gather."]]],
  "Battle of Puebla": ["battle-of-puebla","6e653f5717",[["The French form up below the forts","Lorencez drew up an attack column of zouaves and guns, with a reserve behind it and a convoy guarded on the flank. The forts of Loreto and Guadalupe were held by Negrete's division, and Mexican cavalry stood on the French right."],["The French halt and shell the forts","The zouaves climbed towards Guadalupe and, when opposite it, halted and planted their artillery. The guns fired on both forts, and Zaragoza gave orders for them to be reinforced."],["The first ascent is turned back","The column met two Mexican battalions halfway up, then turned towards the gap between the forts and came under fire from both. Berriozábal was sent to reinforce the hills, and the French fell back under attack from infantry and cavalry."],["The French retreat and a second column comes up","The zouaves retreated and the Mexicans pursued them. The pursuit stopped when another French column came to support the retreating troops."],["The second attack, from the north and the east","The two French columns pushed on together towards the Guadalupe and Resurrection chapel, joined and split into three. The attack was more vigorous. Diaz came up with part of his brigade and two guns."],["The columns are repulsed","The columns that went up from the north were routed. Diaz checked and drove away the French columns marching against the Mexican positions, and the third column, which reached the east side as the others were repulsed, was also defeated."],["The Mexican cavalry charge and the French withdraw","The Mexican cavalry charged the remaining French and prevented them from reorganising for further assaults. The two armies faced each other until seven in the evening, when the French returned to their camp."]]],
  "Battle of Pydna": ["battle-of-pydna","286ac87557",[["The armies stand drawn up","The Romans put their two legions in the middle with allied infantry on the flanks and cavalry on the wings. The Macedonian phalanx held the centre, with the Guard on its left, light troops on both flanks and Perseus with the cavalry on the right."],["The two centres engage","The Macedonians advanced on the Romans a short distance from the Roman camp. The Roman allies tried to beat down the pikes or hack off their points, but with little success."],["The allies retreat and the phalanx loses its line","Unable to get under the thick bristle of pikes, the Romans used a planned retreat over the rough ground. As the phalanx pushed on into the foothills the ground became uneven and its line lost cohesion."],["Paullus sends the legions into the gaps","Paullus ordered the legions into the gaps and they attacked the phalangites on their exposed flanks. At close quarters the longer Roman sword and heavier shield prevailed over the lighter Macedonian arms."],["The Roman right routs the Macedonian left","The Roman right joined the legions after it had routed the Macedonian left. Seeing the battle turn, Perseus fled with the cavalry on the Macedonian right."],["The Guard fights to the death","The 3,000-strong Guard fought to the death. Nearly 11,000 Macedonians were captured, and the pursuit lasted until nightfall, though the battle itself lasted about an hour."]]],
  "Battle of Raphia": ["battle-of-raphia","b8b43f0eb9",[["The two armies drawn up","After five days of skirmishing both kings arrayed their troops. Each put the phalanx in the centre, the light troops and mercenaries beside it, the elephants in front of them and the cavalry on the wings."],["The elephants charge and the Ptolemaic ones panic","The elephants on both wings moved to charge. The smaller Ptolemaic African elephants retreated in panic before the larger Indian ones and ran through the infantry behind them, which fell into disorder."],["Antiochus charges the Ptolemaic horse","Antiochus led his cavalry to the right, rode past the left wing of the Ptolemaic elephants and charged the enemy horse."],["The phalanxes engage, and the Ptolemaic right wins","The two phalanxes closed in a stiff and chaotic fight. Ptolemy rode to the centre to urge on his phalanx, which had Egyptians beside the Macedonians. On the far right the Ptolemaic cavalry routed its opponents."],["Antiochus routs the Ptolemaic horse and pursues","Antiochus routed the Ptolemaic horse facing him and pursued the fleeing enemy in a mass, believing that he had won the day."],["The Ptolemaic phalanx drives the Seleucids back","The Ptolemaic phalanx drove the Seleucid phalanx back. Antiochus tried to ride back, but by then his troops were routed and could not be regrouped, and the battle was over."]]],
  "Battle of Sagrajas": ["battle-of-sagrajas","144f0bc4c0",[["The armies face each other across the river","The two armies had camped facing each other with only the Wadi Bira between them. The Muslims put heavy cavalry in front and kept the Almoravid reserve hidden behind a high mound; Alfonso drew up two divisions and a vanguard."],["The Castilian first division is thrown back","Alfonso sent his first division against Al-Mu'tamid, but it met the Almoravid cavalry before reaching him. The Almoravid archers helped drive it back to its second line, though the Almoravids lost men."],["Alfonso attacks and Sir's battalion is sent","Alfonso then attacked the Almoravids with Al-Mu'tamid. Dawud ibn Aisha sent word to Yusuf ibn Tashfin, who dispatched a battalion that broke into the Castilian army and linked up with Al-Mu'tamid's forces."],["Alfonso reaches the tents and the vanguard gives way","Alfonso kept up the pressure until he reached the Almoravid tents and breached the trench round them. Al-Mu'tamid's Andalusian forces were driven back, and the Castilians went after them."],["The reserve strikes the Castilian camp","While the Castilians chased Al-Mu'tamid, Yusuf ibn Tashfin brought up his reserve and attacked their camp. The Castilian horses panicked at the camels, which he set ablaze, and the camp's remaining men fled towards Alfonso."],["Alfonso turns back and the Muslims counter-attack","Alfonso gave up the pursuit and withdrew to save his camp. Al-Mu'tamid ordered an attack, and Sir ibn Abi Bakr's men charged Alfonso's forces as they fell back."],["The Takrur cavalry charge and Alfonso leaves the field","Yusuf ibn Tashfin ordered 4,000 black cavalry from Takrur to charge. Their charge broke the Christian cavalry, and Alfonso retreated with a small contingent to a nearby hill and escaped after dark."]]],
  "Battle of Salamis": ["battle-of-salamis","31dd3f5f96",[["The Persians block the southern exit","The Allied fleet lay off Salamis, the Athenians on the left and the Spartans probably on the right. The Persian fleet was sent out to block the exit from the straits, and about 400 troops stood on Psyttaleia."],["The Persians pivot into the straits","The Persians seem to have swung their fleet round off Cape Vavari, from a line across the exit to a line running north to south. The Phoenicians were on the right by Mount Aigaleo and the Ionians on the left."],["The Allies back their ships away","As the Persians came on, cramped and disordered in the narrow water, the Allies seemed to back away as if in fear. Plutarch gave the reason as a better position and time to wait for the early morning wind."],["One ship rams and the whole line follows","A single ship shot forward to ram the nearest Persian vessel, and the whole Greek line followed it towards the disordered Persian line. The Athenians and the Aeginetans each claimed the first ship as theirs."],["The Phoenicians are pushed back and the centre is split","The first Persian line fouled the second and third behind it. The leaderless Phoenician squadrons were pushed back against the coast, many running aground, and in the centre a wedge of Greek ships split the Persian fleet in two."],["The Persian fleet retreats towards Phalerum","The Persian fleet began to retreat towards Phalerum, and the Aeginetans ambushed it as it tried to leave the straits. The remaining ships limped back to the harbour and the shelter of the Persian army."],["Aristides destroys the garrison on Psyttaleia","After the Persian fleet had limped back, the Athenian general Aristides took a detachment across to Psyttaleia to slaughter the garrison that Xerxes had left there."]]],
  "Battle of San Jacinto": ["battle-of-san-jacinto","ab3725f9c4",[["The armies face each other across the grass","Houston drew up the Texian line with the second regiment on the left, Burleson in the centre, the artillery and Millard's companies on the right and the cavalry on the extreme right. The Mexican troops lay behind breastworks in their camp."],["The cavalry go wide and the guns move up","The Texian cavalry was sent to the Mexican far left, and the artillery advanced through the tall grass to within 200 yards of the breastworks."],["The Twin Sisters fire and the Texians rush the camp","The Twin Sisters fired and began the battle. After a single volley the Texians broke ranks and swarmed over the breastworks, and the surprised Mexican officers shouted conflicting orders."],["The breastworks fall and the Mexican troops flee","The Texian infantry advanced until they held the woodland and the breastworks. Within 18 minutes the Mexican soldiers left their camp and fled. Santa Anna escaped towards Vince's Bridge."],["Pursuit to Peggy Lake","Many Mexican soldiers retreated through the marsh to Peggy Lake, where Texian riflemen stationed themselves on the banks and shot at anything that moved. The killing lasted for hours."]]],
  "Battle of Sedan (1870)": ["battle-of-sedan-1870","e593726a81",[["The French held a ring round Sedan","MacMahon placed the 7th Corps on the north-west crest, the 1st Corps facing east and the 12th Corps in Bazeilles, with the 5th Corps in reserve in the centre. The Germans closed a ring round the north-west, west and east."],["The Bavarians storm Bazeilles","A Bavarian brigade crossed the Meuse on pontoon bridges in the morning mist and rushed Bazeilles. The French fought from stone houses while Bavarian guns shelled the buildings."],["Wimpffen attacks the Saxons at La Moncelle","Ducrot ordered a retreat but Wimpffen overruled him and threw his forces against the Saxons at La Moncelle. The French rallied briefly, then the counter-attack began to collapse as fresh Prussian troops arrived."],["French cavalry charge at Floing","Ducrot sent Margueritte's cavalry to open an escape route with three attacks on Floing, where the XI Corps stood. Margueritte was mortally wounded in the first charge and German infantry fire defeated the other two."],["The Germans close in and the Bois de la Garenne falls","German infantry reached the Calvaire and attacked from all sides. The 7th Corps broke and the 1st Corps was routed by artillery. When the Guards infantry captured the Bois de la Garenne at 14:30, the French inside surrendered."],["A last attack after the white flag","Napoleon III hoisted a white flag on the walls of Sedan. Wimpffen refused to surrender and launched one more attack, which the Germans defeated."]]],
  "Battle of Shanghai": ["battle-of-shanghai","a54056d239",[["The Chinese assault on the Japanese garrison in the city","Chinese divisions attacked the Japanese naval landing force in Zhabei and towards the Huangpu docks, and the 36th Division joined them. The Japanese Third Fleet shelled the attackers from the river, and the Chinese were halted short of the water."],["Japanese troops land on the coast","The Japanese 3rd and 11th Divisions landed on the north-east coast under naval bombardment, and the Chinese brought troops from the city to meet them. The front line was lengthened from the city to the coast."],["The fight for Luodian","The Chinese concentrated around Luodian and the Japanese came inland against them with naval gunfire, tanks and aircraft. The Chinese were bled almost dry and gave up the town by the end of September."],["The Japanese reach the Wusong Creek","The Chinese fell back to the south bank of the Wusong Creek, and the Japanese attacked across it. The line was breached after two days at one bridge, but the Japanese met further defences behind it."],["The Guangxi counteroffensive","The Guangxi Army joined the line and counterattacked to retake the banks of the creek. It was poorly coordinated and bogged down, and the Japanese answered with artillery, tanks and poison gas."],["Dachang falls and the Chinese leave the city","The Japanese pushed on to Dachang and took it. The Chinese then withdrew from downtown Shanghai, which they had held for almost three months, to a line behind the Suzhou Creek."],["The Japanese cross the creek and land at Jinshanwei","The Japanese crossed the Suzhou Creek, and the Chinese were in danger of encirclement. On 5 November the Japanese 10th Army landed at Jinshanwei on Hangzhou Bay, behind the Chinese line."]]],
  "Battle of Stalingrad": ["battle-of-stalingrad","6a7f6b59c7",[["The 6th Army reaches Stalingrad","The 6th Army reached the outskirts of Stalingrad in pursuit of the Soviet 62nd and 64th Armies, which fell back into the city. The 4th Panzer Army had turned northwards to help take the city from the south. Romanian armies guarded the German flanks."],["The Germans push the defenders to the river","The Germans advanced into the city at great cost and pushed the Soviet defenders back into narrow zones along the west bank of the Volga."],["Operation Uranus overruns the Romanian 3rd Army","Three Soviet armies struck the northern flank of the 6th Army. The Romanian 3rd Army, with thin lines and outnumbered and poorly equipped, was overrun."],["A second attack comes from the south","A second Soviet offensive of two armies was launched south of Stalingrad against the Romanian 4th Army Corps. The Romanian infantry were overrun by large numbers of tanks."],["The ring closes at Kalach","The two Soviet forces raced west and met at Kalach, sealing the ring around Stalingrad. The 6th Army and the 4th Panzer Army were left inside it."],["The relief force advances from Kotelnikovo","Operation Winter Storm was left with the LVII Panzer Corps around Kotelnikovo, supported by the rest of the 4th Panzer Army. By 18 December it had pushed to within 48 km of the 6th Army. The trapped army made no attempt to break out or link up."],["The ring tightens and the 6th Army capitulates","Operation Koltso opened on 10 January 1943 with a very large bombardment, and the Germans retreated from the suburbs to the city. On 2 February the 6th Army capitulated, having exhausted its ammunition and food."]]],
  "Battle of Tarawa": ["battle-of-tarawa","ddda8b725c",[["The warships bombard Betio","Warships and aircraft shelled the island for about three hours before the landing. The Marines waited in the lagoon to land on the north beaches, while the defenders sat in their pillboxes and firing pits."],["The landing is pinned down at the sea wall","The tide was too low for the Higgins boats, and only the tracked LVTs crossed the reef. They could not clear the sea wall, so the first waves were pinned against it. Defenders from the south beaches were shifted to the north."],["By nightfall the line is half-way across","Tanks that reached shore started the line moving, and by nightfall it was about half-way across the island. Ryan gathered men from other waves and took them to a more lightly defended part of Green Beach."],["The west is cleared and the south coast pressed","Ryan's force cleared the western end with naval gunfire, and Marines crossed the airstrip to the south side. On 22 November the 1st Battalion, 6th Marines attacked along the south coast, pushing the defenders east. Part of the 6th landed on Bairiki."],["The Japanese counterattack at night","The Japanese formed up for a counterattack at about 19:30, but artillery broke it up. A banzai charge followed in the early hours of 23 November and killed 45 Americans before destroyers and artillery drove it back."],["The Marines attack both pockets","The 3rd Battalion, 6th Marines relieved the first battalion and attacked the eastern end. The 3rd Battalion, 2nd and the 1st Battalion, 8th closed on the pocket between Red 1 and Red 2 from west and east."],["The island is declared secure","By noon the pocket between Red 1 and Red 2 had been reduced. The 3rd Battalion, 6th Marines reached the eastern tip by 13:00, and the island was declared secure at 13:30."]]],
  "Battle of Thapsus": ["battle-of-thapsus","a44c294427",[["The armies draw up facing each other","Scipio set his legions in three lines in the centre, with cavalry and light infantry on the flanks and elephants in front of them. Caesar also used three lines, with archers and cavalry on the flanks and half of the fifth legion in reserve."],["Caesar's lines advance","A trumpeter of the seventh legion sounded the attack. Caesar saw his right surge forward and ordered a general advance."],["The archers panic the elephants on one flank","Caesar's archers on the right flank attacked the elephants facing them. The animals panicked, turned and trampled their own men."],["The elephants on the other flank charge","The elephants on the other flank charged against Caesar's left. The five cohorts of the fifth legion, placed behind that flank, met the charge."],["The fifth legion turns the elephants back","The legionaries stabbed their pila at the elephants' eyes and weak spots and sounded their trumpets. The beasts turned and ran towards their own lines."],["Scipio's left breaks, then the rest","After the elephants were lost, Scipio began to lose ground. His left broke first and the rest of his army followed, with Caesar's lines pressing after it."],["The siege legions overrun Afranius' camp","The two legions left at the siege marched south to the troops fortified opposite Afranius and Juba's camp. Together they attacked and overran Afranius' camp."]]],
  "Battle of Thermopylae": ["battle-of-thermopylae","2d489a8a37",[["The Greeks hold the middle gate","Leonidas camped at the narrowest part of the pass, where the Phocians had built a wall, and posted 1,000 Phocians on the heights above. The Persian army came up across the Malian Gulf."],["The first day: archers, then the Medes","Xerxes ordered 5,000 archers to shoot, but the arrows were deflected. He then sent 10,000 Medes and Cissians forward in a frontal assault against the Greeks, who fought in front of the wall."],["The Immortals fare no better","Xerxes threw the Immortals into a second assault the same day, and they made no headway. On the second day the Persian infantry again attacked the pass without success."],["Ephialtes shows the mountain path","Xerxes stopped the assault and withdrew to his camp. A Trachinian named Ephialtes then told him of the mountain path, and Hydarnes set out that evening with the Immortals to encircle the Greeks."],["The Persians pass the Phocians","The Phocians guarding the path saw the Persian column and withdrew to a nearby hill to make a stand. The Persians shot a volley of arrows at them and went on with the encirclement."],["The Greeks sally from the wall","Leonidas let his allies leave, and many did. The Spartans and Thespians marched out from the wall into the wider part of the pass and met a Persian force of 10,000 light infantry and cavalry. Leonidas was killed."],["The last stand on the hill","As the Immortals approached, the Greeks withdrew to a hill behind the wall. Most of the Thebans surrendered. Xerxes had part of the wall torn down and the hill surrounded, and the Persians shot arrows until the Greeks on it were dead."]]],
  "Battle of Trafalgar": ["battle-of-trafalgar","522acf5232",[["The fleets close in a ragged line and two columns","The French and Spanish were in a ragged curved line headed north, with the coast behind them. The British came from the west in two columns: Nelson's to the north, Collingwood's to the south."],["Nelson feints at the van and the columns converge","Nelson led his column in a feint toward the van and then turned toward the real point of attack, while Collingwood altered course so that the two columns converged. The foremost British ships came under heavy fire for almost an hour before their guns could bear."],["Royal Sovereign breaks the line","Royal Sovereign outran the rest of the British fleet and came under fire from four allied ships. She then broke the line just astern of the Spanish flagship Santa Ana."],["Victory cuts between Bucentaure and Redoutable","Nelson's flagship Victory cut the line between Villeneuve's flagship Bucentaure and Redoutable. Her broadside through Bucentaure's stern knocked the French flagship out of action, and a general mêlée followed."],["The allied centre and rear are overwhelmed","More British ships entered the battle and the allied centre and rear were gradually overwhelmed. The British ships of both columns stayed engaged with them."],["Dumanoir breaks off with four ships","Dumanoir tried to help the collapsing centre. After failing to fight his way through, he led four French ships away from the fighting. The British took 20 allied vessels and lost none."]]],
  "Battle of Tsushima": ["battle-of-tsushima","2d23bb9994",[["The fleets sight each other","The Russians steamed north-north-east in columns, the main group on the right, the left column and the Nikolai I group to the west and the auxiliaries behind. The Japanese main group came down from the north-east heading south-south-west."],["The Japanese turn in sequence and open fire","Mikasa turned about and the ships behind her followed in sequence to head north-north-east. The Russian main group had shifted left to cover the left column, and the Russians opened fire on the Japanese as they turned."],["The Japanese cross ahead of the Russian column","Mikasa and her line turned north-east and then east to cross the T, and the Russian left column turned north-east and then east-south-east in response. Oslyabya was set on fire, and Knyaz Suvorov caught fire and fell away from the line."],["The Russian main group turns north; the Japanese reverse","With the Japanese overtaking the Russian battleships heading east-south-east, Aleksandr III turned north with Borodino and Oryol following. The 1st division made a U-turn on the spot to head west-north-west, while the 2nd division attacked the secondary Russian warships."],["The Japanese main group catches the Russian main group","After losing sight of the Russian main group in smoke and mist, the 1st division caught up with it heading north-west and fired on the leading Aleksandr III. The 5th division, part of the 3rd Squadron, had been attacking the Russian auxiliaries."],["Torpedo attacks in the night","The 1st division left the battleground and the 2nd and 4th were ordered to gather in the north, leaving the destroyers and torpedo boats to attack in the dark. The attacks forced the Russians to turn west, and they were dispersed in small groups."],["The Russian remnant is surrounded and surrenders","Tōgō's battleships surrounded Nebogatov's remaining squadron, the 1st division approaching from the north. Nebogatov signalled surrender at 10:34, and Tōgō accepted it at 10:45."]]],
  "Battle of Uhud": ["battle-of-uhud","32ebb77ae9",[["The two armies face each other","The Muslims stood on the lower slopes of Uhud with the mountain behind them and 50 archers on a rocky hill to the west. The Meccans formed facing them, with the main body in the middle, a wing on each side and their camp behind."],["The Meccans are pushed back","The Muslims advanced and drove the Meccan army back. Meccan cavalry tried several times to overrun the Muslim left flank, and the archers on the hill turned them away."],["The archers leave the hill","The Muslims pierced the Meccan lines and victory seemed certain. Most of the archers disobeyed Muhammad's order to stay and ran down to join the advance, leaving the flank open."],["Khalid's cavalry attacks the hill and the rear","The archers who had gone down made for the Meccan camp. Khalid ibn al-Walid's cavalry attacked the few archers who had stayed on the hill and then fell on the Muslim flank and rear."],["The Muslims are broken and regroup higher up","Confusion followed and many Muslims were killed, Hamza among them. After hand-to-hand fighting many fled and regrouped higher up the slopes of Uhud, with the Meccan cavalry coming after them."],["Umar's counter-charge clears the slopes","Umar ibn al-Khattab rallied troops at the base of the mountain and led a counter-charge on the Prophet's instruction. It drove Khalid's forces off the slopes, and the Meccan cavalry could not climb them after the Muslims, so the fighting ceased."]]],
  "Battle of Verdun": ["battle-of-verdun","adc6dfd7f0",[["The German guns open on the east bank","After delays from fog, rain and wind, a ten-hour bombardment began on the right bank, and the German corps attacked at 4:00 p.m. French XXX Corps held the north and north-east of the salient."],["The Germans take Bois des Caures","German troops advanced about 5 km and took the wood after two French battalions had held it for two days. The French XXX Corps was forced out of its second line of defence."],["A German party enters Fort Douaumont","Infantry reached their objectives in under twenty minutes, and two parties made for the fort. One of about 100 men found the ditch bunkers unmanned and took prisoner the small maintenance crew inside."],["The attack widens to the west bank","Falkenhayn lent two divisions from the reserve, and the German VI and X Reserve Corps attacked towards Mort-Homme and Côte 304 to silence the French guns across the river. Those guns went on firing into the Germans on the east bank."],["Fort Vaux is taken","The German attack shifted back to the right bank, south of Fort Douaumont. About 10,000 men assaulted Fort Vaux and the top was occupied on 2 June. Fighting continued underground until the garrison surrendered on 7 June."],["The German salient towards Fort Souville","After a gas bombardment the Germans attacked on a front of 5 km and drove a salient of 3 by 2 km into the French defences. They came within 5 km of the Verdun citadel before the French recaptured Chapelle Sainte-Fine and the advance stopped."],["The French offensives regain the ground","After a six-day bombardment French divisions attacked on 20 October and recaptured Fort Douaumont on 24 October. The Germans evacuated Fort Vaux on 2 November, and by 5 November the French were back at the front line of 24 February."]]],
  "Battle of Vienna (1683)": ["battle-of-vienna-1683","21afc85d55",[["The Ottoman army attacks before the allies are deployed","The Ottoman army attacked early, seeking to disrupt the allied deployment. The imperial troops on the left and in the centre met the attack while the Polish troops were still coming into place."],["The imperial army counterattacks in the north","The Germans were the first to counterattack. After heavy fighting and several Ottoman counterattacks, the imperial army took the fortified villages of Nussdorf and Heiligenstadt."],["Polish infantry advances on the Ottoman right","Polish infantry advanced against the Ottoman right flank. Parts of the Ottoman army kept attacking the city, which let the Poles move forward, and by 4:00 pm they held Gersthof."],["The imperial left presses on to the Türkenschanze","The Germans resumed the offensive on the left at 3:30 pm. They met fierce resistance at first, then advanced by 5:00 pm and took Unterdöbling and Oberdöbling, closing on the central Ottoman position."],["Hussars probe the Ottoman line","The Polish cavalry came out of the forest, and a detachment of 120 hussars made a probing charge that showed the Ottomans could be attacked, at the cost of many casualties. The vizier left the Türkenschanze for the main camp."],["The cavalry charges down the hills","The king ordered the cavalry to attack in four contingents. The charge broke the Ottoman lines, the Ottomans retreated, and the horsemen rode for the camps and headquarters while the Viennese garrison sallied out to join in."]]],
  "Battle of Warsaw (1920)": ["battle-of-warsaw-1920","32437aeedc",[["The Red Army's final assault on Warsaw","The Soviet 16th Army attacked at Radzymin, east of Warsaw, while the 3rd and 15th Armies came up to Modlin. The Polish 5th Army had to face three Soviet armies at once."],["Budyonny's army marches on Lwów, not Warsaw","The Soviet High Command ordered the 1st Cavalry Army to advance on Warsaw from the south. It marched on Lwów instead and missed the battle, leaving only a weak Mazyr Group on the southern flank."],["The Polish 5th Army counterattacks in the north","The 5th Army crossed the Wkra and fought the Soviet 3rd and 15th Armies at Nasielsk. The Soviet advance towards Warsaw and Modlin was halted at the end of 15 August."],["The Assault Group breaks through the Mazyr Group","Piłsudski's Assault Group marched north from the Wieprz and met almost no opposition. It routed the Mazyr Group and cut the supply lines of the Soviet 16th Army."],["The Soviet 16th Army is cut off near Białystok","Tukhachevsky ordered a retreat, but his orders came too late or not at all. The Polish 1st Legions Division made a forced march and cut off the 16th Army, and most of its troops surrendered."],["The northern Soviet armies join the general rout","The 15th Army, which had tried to cover the retreat of the 4th Army, was defeated twice and joined the rout. By 21 August all organised Soviet resistance had ceased."]]],
  "Battle of Waterloo": ["battle-of-waterloo","5040b9836c",[["The armies face each other across the Brussels road","Wellington drew up his infantry just behind the crest of the ridge, with Hougoumont held ahead of his right and his heavy cavalry out of sight behind the ridge. The French formed on the slopes of another ridge to the south, symmetrically about the road."],["Hougoumont is attacked and I Corps advances","The grande batterie bombarded the centre and Reille's corps attacked Hougoumont. D'Erlon then advanced his divisions against Wellington's left wing."],["The British heavy cavalry charge","Uxbridge's two heavy brigades crossed the crest and charged downhill. I Corps was driven back across the valley in rout, and some of the cavalry went on to attack the guns of the grande batterie."],["The French cavalry attack the centre","Ney tried to break Wellington's centre with cavalry alone, sending in his reserve cavalry between Hougoumont and La Haye Sainte. Wellington's infantry formed squares against them."],["The Prussians arrive and Lobau is sent to stop them","Bülow's IV Corps came up on the French right with Plancenoit as its objective. Napoleon sent Lobau's corps to stop it."],["The Imperial Guard attacks and I Corps breaks","Napoleon committed the Imperial Guard against Wellington's centre, led by Ney. As it went forward Zieten's Prussian I Corps attacked the French right, and d'Erlon's corps broke and fled."],["The Guard retreats and Wellington advances","The Guard was repulsed and the last of it retreated. Wellington signalled a general advance and his army went forward against the retreating French."]]],
  "Battle of Yamen": ["battle-of-yamen","951536c456",[["The chained Song fleet and the four Yuan parts","The Song ships lay chained in a long string in the bay, with the emperor's boat in the middle. Zhang Hongfan split his ships into four parts: three for the Song's east, north and south sides, and one under his own command a short way off."],["The north flank is repulsed","The Yuan north squadron engaged the Song fleet first and was driven back. The Song ships stayed chained where they lay, and the rest of the Yuan force held its places."],["Zhang Hongfan attacks from the front","After the Yuan played festive music, which led the Song to think they were feasting, Zhang Hongfan attacked from the front with more soldiers hidden under cloth. Waves of arrows hit the Song ships, and seven ships were lost at once."],["Zhang Shijie tries to break out","The chained Song ships could neither support the middle nor retreat, and command broke down. Zhang Shijie cut about a dozen ships from the formation in an attempt to save the emperor."],["The Yuan reach the emperor's boat","The Yuan advanced quickly to the centre and to Zhao Bing, killing everyone in their way. Lu Xiufu took the boy emperor and jumped into the sea, and both drowned. Zhang Shijie escaped the battle."]]],
  "Battle of Zama": ["battle-of-zama","97d584bfee",[["The armies draw up facing each other","The Romans put their heavy infantry in the centre in three ranks, with Laelius's cavalry on the left and Masinissa's Numidians on the right. Hannibal set elephants before two lines of infantry, a third line well behind them, and cavalry on each flank."],["The elephants charge with two lines behind them","The Numidian horsemen on each side skirmished with javelins, then Hannibal sent in the elephants and moved his first two infantry lines forward in support. His third line stayed where it stood."],["The elephants are turned and the cavalry driven off","The velites threw javelins at the elephants and the legions sounded their bugles. Many elephants bolted into the Roman gaps and some back through the Carthaginian cavalry. Masinissa and Laelius charged the disordered horse and chased it away."],["The first lines of infantry clash","With elephants and cavalry gone from the field, all three Roman ranks and the first two Carthaginian lines advanced. The hastati took heavy losses, but the Carthaginian front line broke and fled round the flanks of the second."],["The second line pushes the Romans back","The Carthaginian second line fought fiercely and drove the hastati back in disorder. The Romans committed the principes, and the second line then fled, with the hastati pursuing it. The Carthaginian third line did not move."],["Both sides re-form into single long lines","The Romans recalled the hastati and formed one line, with the principes and triarii at the sides. The Carthaginians rallied survivors of their first two lines to lengthen the veterans' line, so it outflanked the Romans."],["The Roman cavalry returns and takes the line in the rear","The two lines charged each other and neither gained the advantage. Masinissa's and Laelius's cavalry then came back and charged the Carthaginian line from behind. Its line collapsed."]]],
  "Battle of al-Qadisiyyah": ["battle-of-al-qadisiyyah","377aa63230",[["Both armies form up in four divisions","The Sasanian army crossed the canal before dawn and drew up in four divisions with the river at its back. The Rashidun army stood in four corps about 500 metres away, with Muslim cavalry at the rear of each."],["Elephants and archers press the Muslim right","The Persian left wing attacked the Muslim right wing with heavy arrow fire, and the elephants led the charge. Jareer's cavalry was stopped by the Sasanian heavy cavalry, and the Muslim infantry began to fall back."],["The Muslim right counterattacks","Reinforcing infantry from the right centre and a cavalry regiment under Al-Ash'ath joined the right wing. The infantry attacked from the front and the cavalry from the flank, and the Sasanian left wing retreated."],["The Persian right and its elephants drive back the left","Rustam then sent his right centre and right wing forward after heavy arrow fire, with the elephants in the lead. The Muslim cavalry on the left, already in panic at the elephants, was driven back."],["The elephants are turned back","Asim's archers shot the men on the elephants and his infantry cut the saddle girths. The elephants were withdrawn, the Persian right centre and right wing retreated, and the Muslims counterattacked."],["Qa'qa leads the left centre forward","The elephants had been driven from the field on 18 November. On the fourth day the Muslim left centre surged forward and the other corps followed. By noon Qa'qa and his men had pierced the Sasanian centre but could not break the army."],["The Sasanian front collapses","After a last stand the Sasanian front gave way. Part of the army retreated in order and the rest in panic towards the river, while Jalinus held the bridgehead and brought the bulk across. The Muslim cavalry pursued."]]],
  "Battle of the Bulge": ["battle-of-the-bulge","149d281fa9",[["Three German armies face the Ardennes front","The Sixth Panzer Army stood in the north, the Fifth Panzer Army in the middle and the Seventh Army in the south. The U.S. 99th and 2nd Divisions, the 106th Division and VIII Corps held the line opposite them."],["The assault opens and Peiper's group leads the north","The Germans opened with a 90-minute artillery barrage and attacked all along the front. Kampfgruppe Peiper, charged with the main effort, left the Sixth Panzer Army's line late."],["Two regiments of the 106th Division are surrounded","The Fifth Panzer Army surrounded two largely intact regiments of the 106th Division in a pincer movement and forced their surrender."],["The northern shoulder holds and Peiper turns south","The 99th and 2nd Divisions kept the Sixth Panzer Army from the ridge and the roads west of it. Peiper turned south round Hünningen and reached Stavelot, where the Americans delayed him."],["St. Vith slows the Fifth Panzer Army","The defenders of St. Vith, led by the 7th Armored Division, resisted the German attacks and slowed the advance. The 2nd Panzer Division spearheaded the Fifth Panzer Army."],["Bastogne is surrounded","The 101st Airborne Division reached Bastogne by truck on 19 December. The panzer columns swung past on either side and the Germans had surrounded the town by 21 December."],["The advance stops short of the Meuse; Bastogne is relieved","Parts of the 2nd Panzer Division came in sight of the Meuse at Foy-Notre-Dame on 24 December, and the U.S. 2nd Armored Division attacked them there. On 26 December the 4th Armored Division broke through to Bastogne."]]],
  "Battle of the Catalaunian Plains": ["battle-of-the-catalaunian-plains","025f0c46a2",[["The two armies deploy along the ridge","Both sides spent the day deploying their coalition armies. Attila's Huns stood in the centre with their vassals on the wings, while the Romans took the left, the Alans the centre and the Visigoths the right."],["The Romans take the left and repulse the Gepids","Attila's forces reached the right side of the ridge first. The Romans then arrived on the left side and drove back the Gepids as they came up."],["The Alans and Thorismund secure the centre","The Alans and the Visigoths under Thorismund fought their way up the ridge and took its centre, which they held against Attila."],["The Visigoths try to take the right of the ridge","Theodoric led his Visigoths against the Amali Goths on the right. He was killed in the assault, and the rest of his army did not know of his death."],["Thorismund drives back the Hunnic centre","Thorismund found Attila's place in the Hunnic line and attacked the Hunnic centre, nearly killing Attila. The Hunnic centre was forced to retreat."],["Attila withdraws into his camp as night falls","Attila sought refuge in his wagon camp. When night fell, Thorismund, returning towards friendly lines, entered the camp by mistake and was wounded in the fighting there."]]],
  "Battle of the Granicus": ["battle-of-the-granicus","d8a4713d92",[["Both armies drawn up on either side of the river","The Persian cavalry stood along the east bank with the Greek mercenaries behind it. Alexander took the right wing of the Macedonian line and Parmenion the left, and the Persians moved cavalry to their own left when they saw Alexander opposite it."],["Amyntas attacks the Persian left wing","After a moment of silence Alexander ordered Amyntas to attack the Persian left wing, with the squadron of Socrates at the front. The Persians answered the charge with volleys of javelins."],["Amyntas's force is pushed back","Outnumbered, and facing Persians on higher ground at the top of the bank, the vanguard suffered losses and retreated towards Alexander. Alexander now attacked with the remaining Companion cavalry of the right wing."],["Alexander crosses and fights on the far bank","Alexander and his men engaged the Persians and their leaders in a hard-fought fight. Other Macedonian cavalry established a foothold on the bank and reinforced his force."],["The Persian cavalry is driven back and breaks","The Macedonian cavalry and the light infantry among them forced the Persian cavalry back, first on the left where Alexander was fighting. Then the centre broke and the Persian cavalry on both wings fled."],["The Greek mercenaries are attacked from all sides","Alexander did not pursue the fleeing cavalry far. The mercenaries, mostly inactive at their original position, were surprised and attacked by the Foot Companions from the front and the cavalry from all other directions. They were soon defeated and only a few escaped."]]],
  "Battle of the Hydaspes": ["battle-of-the-hydaspes","9e4cec735d",[["The armies draw up","The Indians stood with cavalry on both flanks, chariots in front and infantry in the centre with elephants before it. Alexander judged the centre the strongest part and kept his phalanx back while his cavalry prepared to go for the flanks."],["The Macedonian cavalry strikes the Indian flanks","The Dahae horse archers harassed the Indian right-wing cavalry, and the Companions, with Alexander leading, charged the Indian cavalry on the left wing, which was outnumbered."],["The Indian right-wing cavalry is caught from behind","The rest of the Indian cavalry rode over to help the hard-pressed left wing. Coenus's squadrons followed and struck them from the rear, so the Indian horsemen had to face two attacks at once."],["The Indian cavalry is routed","The Indian horsemen tried to form a double phalanx, but the manoeuvre added to their confusion. They were routed and fled towards the safety of their elephants."],["The elephants advance and meet the phalanx","The war elephants advanced and were met by the Macedonian phalanx. They caused heavy losses among the Macedonian foot, trampling and disorganising their dense lines, but the infantry kept up its resistance."],["The elephants are driven back into their own lines","The elephants were repulsed and fled back to their own lines. Many of their mahouts had been struck down, and the maddened animals trampled many of their own infantry and cavalry."],["The phalanx advances and the cavalry charges from behind","The Macedonian foot locked their shields and advanced on the confused enemy mass while the cavalry charged from the rear. The whole Indian army was routed, and Craterus's force, having crossed the river, pursued the fleeing Indians."]]],
  "Battle of the Little Bighorn": ["battle-of-the-little-bighorn","556b0b51cf",[["Reno crosses the river and charges the village","Reno's three troops forded the Little Bighorn at the mouth of Reno Creek on Custer's orders and rode towards the village. Custer's battalion followed the ridges to the east, while Benteen and the pack train were still behind."],["Reno dismounts and the warriors ride out","Reno stopped a few hundred yards short of the village, fearing a trap, and put his men into a skirmish line. The mounted warriors streamed out and rode against the centre and exposed left end of the line."],["The warriors turn Reno's left and he falls back to the trees","Over 500 warriors massed behind a small hill on Reno's left and attacked his left and rear. Reno withdrew into the timber along the river bend, where the warriors pinned his men down."],["Reno's men break for the bluffs","Reno led his men out of the timber in a disorderly rout along the river. They crossed under attack and climbed the bluffs on the far side, leaving wounded behind in the timber."],["Benteen and the pack train join Reno on the bluffs","Benteen's three troops reached Reno's shaken men while they were still climbing from the valley, and McDougall's troop and the pack train came later. The command dug rifle pits and held an all-round defence."],["Yates' companies appear at the mouth of Medicine Tail Coulee","With Reno driven from the village, Custer moved north and a wing of his battalion under Yates went down to the river at the Minneconjou Ford. Hundreds of warriors left the fight with Reno to protect the village."],["Custer's battalion is overwhelmed on Custer Hill","Custer's companies moved to Custer Hill, where the warriors, led by Crazy Horse by the Lakota account, charged from the northeast. Custer's remaining troops, E, F and half of C, were soon killed."]]],
  "Battle of the Nile": ["battle-of-the-nile","979c4c6c86",[["The French line at anchor, the British closing","The French thirteen ships of the line lay at anchor along the edge of the shoals, with the van near Aboukir Island and Orient at the centre. The British fleet came up in the evening, with Swiftsure and Alexander out of sight to the west."],["Goliath and Zealous cross the head of the line","Foley saw a gap between Guerrier and the shoal and took Goliath through it, and Zealous followed. Orion, Theseus and Audacious also passed inside the line, which was ready to fight only on its seaward side."],["The seaward ships anchor and Culloden grounds","Vanguard, Minotaur and Defence anchored on the seaward side of the French van, which was now caught on both sides. Bellerophon and Majestic went on to the French centre. Culloden ran onto the shoal near Aboukir Island."],["Bellerophon drifts out and the last ships join","Bellerophon, battered by Orient, cut her anchor cables at 20:20 and drifted away from the fight. Swiftsure and Alexander came up in the dark and opened fire on Orient and Franklin."],["The French van surrenders","Guerrier, Conquérant and Spartiate struck their colours, and Aquilon was dismasted and its officers surrendered at 21:25. Peuple Souverain had already cut its cable and drifted south, and surrendered in the night."],["Orient explodes and the centre gives way","A fire on Orient reached her magazines at 22:00 and the ship blew up. Tonnant, Heureux and Mercure had already cut their cables and drifted south. Franklin fought on after the lull and was forced to surrender."],["The French rear stands out to sea","Firing began again between the French southern ships and Alexander and Majestic. Heureux and Mercure had drifted onto the shoal and surrendered, Tonnant was driven ashore, and the surviving French ships stood out to sea."]]],
  "Battle of the Plains of Abraham": ["battle-of-the-plains-of-abraham","508df425cd",[["Wolfe's line forms on the plateau","Wolfe's army formed a line across the Plains, its right on the bluff above the St Lawrence and its left on a bluff and wood above the St Charles. Canadian militia and native sharpshooters engaged the left flank from the trees and scrub."],["Montcalm orders a general advance","The French regulars moved against the British line. The British lines held their fire as the French approached."],["The British fire two volleys and the French break","The French fired two disorganised volleys. The British then fired at close range, marched forward a few paces and fired a second general volley, which sent the attackers into retreat."],["The French retreat and the Highlanders pursue","British troops fell into a disorganised pursuit of the retreating French. The 78th Fraser Highlanders followed with their swords and met heavy fire from the militia that remained in the trees."],["Bougainville's column arrives from the rear","Townshend took charge and saw Bougainville's column approaching from the British rear. He formed two battalions from the confused troops and turned them to meet it."],["Bougainville retreats and the French cross the St Charles","Bougainville did not attack and retreated. The rest of Montcalm's army slipped back across the St Charles, the militia holding the bridge."]]],
  "Battle of the Pyramids": ["battle-of-the-pyramids","a315d755e2",[["The two armies take their places","The French formed five divisional squares in echelon, Desaix on the right and Bon on the left beside the Nile. Murad Bey fixed his right at fortified Embabeh and his left at Biktil, with the Mamluk cavalry between them."],["The French advance and the cavalry charge the squares","The French moved south in echelon and Desaix sent a detachment to occupy Biktil. Murad meant to hold them on his fortified flanks and strike their centre with cavalry, and repeated charges were made against the squares."],["The mass assault of about 15:30","Murad ordered his defterdar, Ayyub Bey, to lead a mass assault. The divisions of Desaix, Reynier and Dugua held and repelled the horsemen with musketry and artillery, and some Mamluks who attacked Desaix’s detachment failed."],["Bon’s columns storm Embabeh","Near the river Bon’s division formed attack columns and stormed Embabeh. The garrison broke and many fled into the Nile, where hundreds drowned."],["Murad escapes towards Upper Egypt","Murad’s losses were far heavier than the French, with thousands killed or wounded, Ayyub Bey among them. Murad, wounded in the cheek, got away with several thousand cavalry to Upper Egypt."]]],
  "Battle of the Somme": ["battle-of-the-somme","5babce95d4",[["The corps stand along the front","From north to south the British VII, VIII, X, III, XV and XIII Corps and the French Sixth Army faced three corps of the German 2nd Army. The Reserve Army's cavalry waited west of Albert."],["The first day: success in the south, disaster in the north","The French, XIII Corps and XV Corps took their objectives, and a German retreat began south of the Somme. North of the Albert–Bapaume road the attacks failed, or won ground that was mostly lost again."],["The Fourth Army attacks the second position","Four divisions attacked at 3:25 a.m. after a five-minute bombardment on a front of 6,000 yards. Most of the objective was captured, but the attack was not followed up."],["The Reserve Army takes Pozières","The 1st Australian Division of the Reserve Army captured Pozières, the only British success of a general attack on 22 and 23 July. German bombardments and counter-attacks continued until 7 August."],["The third general offensive and the attack on Combles","The British attacked an intermediate line and the German third line, while a French attack aimed to encircle Combles. The front line moved forward by 2,500 to 3,500 yards, but no breakthrough came."],["Morval and Thiepval Ridge","The Fourth Army attacked Morval, Gueudecourt and Lesboeufs, held by the German 1st Army, and took them with Combles, while the French made slower progress. The Reserve Army began its attack on Thiepval Ridge a day later."],["The Fifth Army attacks into the Ancre valley","The Fifth Army, as the Reserve Army was now named, attacked into the Ancre valley. Beaumont Hamel and Beaucourt were captured, though the attack on Serre failed. It was the last big British operation of the year."]]],
  "Battle of the Teutoburg Forest": ["battle-of-the-teutoburg-forest","910b19a07f",[["The Bructeri strike the strung-out column","The Roman army moved through thick forest, its line of march stretched over 15 to 20 kilometres. The Bructeri attacked along its length with javelins, a brief skirmish meant to tire the Romans and damage their supplies. The Angrivarii lay hidden at Kalkriese."],["The Bructeri withdraw and the Romans camp","The Bructeri withdrew quickly. A torrential downpour left the Romans unable to advance, and Varus ordered the army to make camp. Losses reported were light, but the baggage trains and scout cavalry were shown to be vulnerable."],["A night march into the second trap","The Romans marched at night to escape and went into another trap set at the foot of Kalkriese Hill. There the open strip they could march along was squeezed to a gap of about 100 metres between the woods and the swampland at the edge of the Great Bog."],["The Romans try to storm the wall","The road was blocked by a trench and an earthen wall built along its side, which let the Germanic alliance attack from cover. The Romans made a desperate attempt to storm the wall and failed."],["Vala rides off and is overtaken","The highest-ranking officer next to Varus, Numonius Vala, abandoned the troops by riding off with the cavalry. Germanic cavalry overtook him and he was killed shortly afterwards, according to Velleius Paterculus."],["The Germanic warriors storm the field","The Germanic warriors stormed the field and cut down the disintegrating Roman forces. Varus killed himself. By later accounts some officers were sacrificed or ransomed and some common soldiers enslaved."]]],
  "Battle of the Trebia": ["battle-of-the-trebia","ebb43d2c4b",[["The armies form up on the west bank","After the Romans waded the Trebia, both armies drew up symmetrically, infantry in the centre and cavalry on the wings. Mago's force had been put in a watercourse to the south in the night and stayed out of sight."],["The Roman cavalry flees and the infantry lines close","On each wing 5,000 Carthaginian horse charged the 2,000 Roman horse, which fled back over the Trebia. Most of the Carthaginian cavalry pursued them while the heavy infantry lines met in the centre."],["Carthaginian skirmishers go round the exposed flank","The Carthaginian light infantry moved forward and round the Roman flank, which the flight of the cavalry had left open. The velites turned to face them and many allied infantry on the flanks turned too, which slowed their push on the front."],["Mago's force strikes the Roman left rear","Mago's men came down the watercourse unnoticed and charged the velites, who were already holding off the Carthaginian light infantry. The velites held their formation."],["The Carthaginian cavalry returns to the Roman rear","Increasing numbers of Carthaginian cavalry broke off the pursuit, came back and attacked the Roman rear. Under this strain the allied infantry on the flanks and the velites began to break up."],["The Roman centre breaks through the Gauls","The Roman infantry in the centre routed the 8,000 Gauls facing them and broke through the middle of the Carthaginian army. Behind them the rest of the Roman army had dissolved."],["Ten thousand Romans reach Placentia","Sempronius ordered the infantry away from the field. Keeping its formation, 10,000 of them recrossed the Trebia and reached Placentia. The Carthaginians cut down the partly surrounded rest of the Roman army."]]],
  "Battle of the Yarmuk": ["battle-of-the-yarmuk","18f269803f",[["The two armies stand facing each other","The Byzantines faced east and the Muslims west, on fronts of about 13 and 12 kilometres. Each side had four sections, and each Byzantine army kept its cavalry in the rear. On the first day a third of the Byzantine infantry attacked and neither side gained ground."],["The Byzantine wings drive the Muslim wings back","The Byzantine centre only pinned the Muslim centre. On the wings Qanatir's Slavs and Gregory's army attacked in force, and the Muslim wings fell back towards their camps."],["Khalid's guard strikes the Byzantine left wing","Khalid sent the mobile guard against the southern flank of the Byzantine left wing while the Muslim right wing attacked from the front. The Byzantine left wing gave up the ground it had won and Amr regained his position."],["Qanatir presses the right again and Khalid flanks the centre","Qanatir led his Slavs, with help from Armenians and Jabalah's Christian Arabs, against the Muslim right wing and right centre, which fell back. Khalid sent the mobile guard at the flanks of the Byzantine left centre while Abu Ubayda and Yazid attacked on the left."],["Dhirar secures the bridge in the night","Khalid had grouped all his cavalry into one force of about 8,000, with the mobile guard as its core. He sent Dhirar with 500 cavalry round the Byzantine northern flank to take the bridge at Ayn Dhakar, the best crossing of the western ravine."],["The cavalry sweeps round the left and routs the horse","Khalid galloped his cavalry round the Byzantine left wing and attacked its rear while the Muslim right wing pressed from the front, and the left wing collapsed. He then wheeled back upon the Byzantine cavalry as it formed and routed it to the north."],["The Byzantine centre breaks and the army is trapped","Khalid's cavalry fell on the Byzantine left centre from behind and broke it, and a general retreat began. The Byzantines fell back west towards the bridge, which Dhirar's men already held, while Khalid took his cavalry north to block that way out."]]],
  "Battle on the Ice": ["battle-on-the-ice","31b4b3abf3",[["The two armies stand on the lake","Alexander and the men of Novgorod drew up their forces by the lake. The crusaders stood opposite, ready to ride at them. The wings were part of Alexander's army."],["The crusaders charge across the lake","The knights and crusaders charged across the lake and reached the enemy. The chronicle of Novgorod says they drove themselves like a wedge through the Rus' army."],["The militia holds them up","The infantry of the Novgorodian militia held up the charge and its momentum slowed. For a little more than two hours the two sides fought at close quarters on the frozen surface."],["Alexander sends in the wings","After a little more than two hours Alexander ordered the left and right wings of his army, cavalry among them, into the battle. The crusaders were by then exhausted from the struggle on the slippery ice."],["The crusaders fall back in disarray","The crusaders started to retreat in disarray, deeper onto the ice, with the fresh Novgorod cavalry now facing them."],["The retreat becomes a panic","The appearance of the fresh Novgorod cavalry made the crusaders retreat in panic. The Novgorod First Chronicle says the fighting went on during the pursuit, seven versts short of the Subol shore."]]],
  "Battles of Khalkhin Gol": ["battles-of-khalkhin-gol","73eef2dc14",[["The Soviet attack opens with guns and aircraft","The Soviet and Mongolian force stood west of the Khalkhin Gol with armour on both wings. The Japanese 23rd Infantry Division held the east bank. Artillery and aircraft opened the attack."],["The Soviet centre crosses and pins the Japanese","Three infantry divisions and a tank brigade crossed the river and attacked the east bank, supported by the guns. The Japanese were held in place while the armour on the wings prepared to move."],["The armour sweeps round both flanks","Soviet armoured units swept around the flanks of the Japanese and came at them from the rear. Each wing moved round its own side of the Japanese line while the centre held them in front."],["The wings meet at Nomonhan","The two Soviet wings joined near Nomonhan, which closed a ring behind the Japanese 23rd Infantry Division and trapped it."],["The relief and the break-out fail","A Japanese counterattack to relieve the 23rd Division failed on 26 August. On 27 August the division tried to break out of the encirclement and also failed."],["The encircled force is hit again and broken up","The surrounded Japanese would not surrender and were hit again with artillery and air attacks. By 31 August the Japanese on the Mongolian side of the border were destroyed."]]],
  "Battles of Lexington and Concord": ["battles-of-lexington-and-concord","38d57e9120",[["The militia stand on Lexington Common","About 80 Lexington militiamen formed up on the Common in plain sight, but not across the road to Concord. Pitcairn's advance guard entered Lexington, with Smith's remaining companies further down the road."],["The Regulars charge and the militia disperse","After shots were fired, of which the accounts disagree on the first, the Regulars charged with bayonets. Eight Lexington men were killed and ten wounded, and the militia fell back."],["The Regulars search Concord","Smith reformed the column and marched to Concord, where he divided it to search for supplies. Four companies under Captain Parsons went beyond the North Bridge, while Laurie's companies guarded it. Barrett's militia watched from a hill to the north."],["The fight at the North Bridge","Barrett's militia advanced on the bridge and exchanged fire with Laurie's companies, who broke and fled to the grenadiers coming from the town. This isolated the four companies at the farm."],["The column leaves Concord and is hit on the road","Parsons' companies returned to the town and the Regulars marched east. Militia converged on the road and the British charged up Brooks Hill, but the colonists held it and inflicted heavy casualties on the attackers."],["Percy's brigade rescues the column at Lexington","Near the Lexington line Parker's men, some bandaged, ambushed the column from a hill, and Smith was wounded. Percy's brigade arrived with artillery and fired at extreme range, dispersing the pursuing militia."],["Percy's column fights its way to Charlestown","The combined column set out with flankers on both sides and the rearguard rotated. Militia fought it all along the road, notably at Jason Russell's farm in Menotomy. Percy turned onto a track to Charlestown, and the circle of fire was broken."]]],
  "Fall of Constantinople": ["fall-of-constantinople","dc73de39ff",[["The armies take their positions","The Ottoman army camped outside the land walls. The European regulars lay along the walls, the troops from Anatolia south of the Lycus, and the guns and Janissaries near the Mesoteichion. Constantine held the Mesoteichion and Giustiniani the Charisian Gate to the north."],["The guns open fire and the chain holds the fleet out","Mehmed's cannons fired on the walls for weeks, but the Byzantines repaired most of the damage after each shot. The Ottoman fleet made probing attacks but could not enter the Golden Horn because of the chain."],["Ships are hauled over the hill into the Horn","Mehmed had a road of greased logs laid across Galata and dragged his ships over the hill into the Golden Horn, bypassing the chain. This threatened the supply ships from Pera and demoralised the defenders."],["The fire-ship attack on the hauled ships fails","The defenders tried to destroy the Ottoman ships in the Golden Horn with fire ships. The Ottomans forced the Christians to retreat with many casualties."],["The final assault begins at the Blachernae walls","The Christian troops of the Ottoman Empire attacked first, followed by irregular azaps and Anatolian Turkmen, who focused on a damaged section of the Blachernae walls. The Turkmen breached it and entered the city but were quickly pushed back."],["The Janissaries attack and the Genoese give way","The last wave, the elite Janissaries, attacked the walls. Giustiniani was grievously wounded and his evacuation caused panic among the defenders. His Genoese retreated into the city and towards the harbour, while Constantine's men held their ground."],["The Ottomans enter the city","The defenders were overwhelmed at several points along the wall, and the Janissaries pressed forward. The Venetians retreated to their ships, which left the Horn, and the Ottoman navy held the Golden Horn by midday."]]],
  "Fall of Singapore": ["fall-of-singapore","d405d5732b",[["The island divided into three sectors","Two Australian brigades held the west, where the invasion was most likely, III Indian Corps held the north-east and the Southern Area forces the south-east. The Japanese stood across the Johor Strait."],["The 5th and 18th Divisions land in the north-west","The first wave crossed the strait shortly before 20:30. The main weight, about 13,000 men, fell on the 22nd Australian Brigade, which numbered just 3,000."],["The 22nd Brigade falls back towards the centre","The Australian battalions that had been engaged regrouped and moved back east from the coast, and the Japanese divisions followed inland. The Australians were pushed back further in the early morning."],["The Imperial Guards attack the 27th Brigade","The Guards crossed on the 27th Brigade's front, near the causeway, and some reached the shore. Mortars, machine-guns and burning oil on the water caused them severe casualties, and Yamashita ordered them to press on."],["The 27th Brigade withdraws from the causeway","Maxwell could not contact the 22nd Brigade and feared encirclement. His brigade withdrew east of the Bukit Timah Road, and the Allies lost the beaches by the causeway. Bennett later said he had not authorised the move."],["The Japanese push on to Bukit Panjang and Bukit Timah","The 5th Division aimed at Bukit Panjang and the 18th Division at Bukit Timah. The 18th fell on the 22nd Brigade's area, and the Japanese captured the Bukit Timah area with the garrison's main food and fuel depots."],["The Commonwealth forces pull back to a city perimeter","The Commonwealth forces withdrew from the northern shore and Changi to a perimeter around Singapore City at the eastern end of the island, with harassing attacks all the way. The 44th Brigade held from Buona Vista to Pasir Panjang."]]],
  "Fall of Tenochtitlan": ["fall-of-tenochtitlan","140e762d86",[["The three camps and the sloops take their places","Cortés sent Alvarado to Tacuba, Olid to Coyohuacan and Sandoval to Iztapalapa, each with Spanish troops and about 8,000 allies, and kept the thirteen sloops under his own command on the lake. The Mexica held the city with their canoes."],["Alvarado and Olid are pushed back at Tlacopan","Alvarado and Olid marched to Chapultepec to cut the city's spring water, then tried to cross the Tlacopan causeway. The Mexica drove them back with attacks by land and by canoe."],["The sloops meet the canoes on the lake","Cortés launched the thirteen sloops from Texcoco and met more than a thousand canoes. A favourable breeze let him overturn many of them and kill or capture many of their crews."],["A gap is opened and the sloops are shared out","After the first battle on the lake Cortés camped with Olid's forces. He opened a gap in the causeway for the sloops and gave four to Alvarado, six to Olid and two to Sandoval, so that the canoes could no longer attack from the lake side."],["The camps hold the causeways and creep forward","The Spaniards stayed on the causeways at night, since the Mexica occupied any bridge they left. They razed the districts they took and used the rubble to fill the canals and gaps, which let them advance closer to the city."],["Cuauhtemoc attacks all three camps","Cuauhtemoc attacked all three Spanish camps at once with his entire army. The Spanish kept their camps on the causeways."],["The camps close on Tlatelolco and the canoes are cut off","The three camps advanced together towards the Tlatelolco marketplace, and the defending army was destroyed. As hundreds of canoes fled the city, Cortés sent the sloops to intercept them."]]],
  "First Battle of the Marne": ["first-battle-of-the-marne","44cd5bb2c0",[["The 6th Army meets the German right flank","French reservists of the 6th Army, probing northeast of Paris, met the German IV Reserve Corps near the Ourcq. Though outnumbered, the corps attacked and held them for 24 hours before it retreated."],["Kluck turns west and the 5th Army attacks Bülow","Kluck turned his army to face west against the 6th Army and moved off its attacks. The right wing of the 5th Army attacked the German 2nd Army and pinned it, while the turn left a gap between the two German armies."],["Bülow's right gives way while Hausen attacks Foch","Bülow ordered his right wing to retreat while his left wing attacked with Hausen's 3rd Army. Hausen's night attack on Foch's 9th Army in the marshes pushed it back 13 km before it bogged down."],["The BEF and the 5th Army enter the gap","The Allies sent the BEF northwest toward Kluck and the 5th Army northeast toward Bülow, into the gap between the two German armies. The BEF crossed the Petit Morin and established a bridgehead over the Marne."],["Quast breaks through the 6th Army","Kluck had telegraphed that an enveloping attack by General von Quast would bring the decision. Quast fought his way through the 6th Army's defences, and the way to Paris, 50 km away, lay open."],["Hentsch's order turns the 1st and 2nd Armies back","Hentsch agreed with Bülow that a general retreat was needed once the Allies crossed the Marne. On 9 September Bülow withdrew again, and Kluck, with Bülow retreating, had no choice but to follow."],["The Germans fall back to the Aisne","Moltke ordered the 3rd, 4th and 5th Armies back as well, to the Aisne. The Germans retreated 65 km and dug in north of the river, pursued by the French and British at a slow pace."]]],
  "Normandy landings": ["normandy-landings","0bb471997a",[["Airborne troops land behind the beaches","Pathfinders of the US airborne divisions arrived at 00:15, and British glider troops took the Caen canal and Orne bridges at 00:16. Many paratroopers landed far from their drop zones."],["The flanks are taken","On the western side the 82nd Airborne captured the crossroads at Sainte-Mère-Église within two hours of landing. On the eastern side the 6th Airborne held the bridges over the Caen canal and the Orne."],["The fleet shells the coast","Warships bombarded the ground behind the beaches in the dark and, from 05:50, pre-assigned targets on the beaches themselves. The assault divisions crossed in landing craft, which strong winds blew east of their intended positions."],["The five beaches are landed on","The 4th Division landed at Utah at 06:30, south of its intended place. Omaha's obstacles were largely undamaged and its fire from the cliffs was heavy. Gold was landed from 07:25, Sword from 07:30, and Juno after a delay."],["The beachheads push inland","Combat teams left Utah from about 09:00 and met the airborne troops. At Omaha barely 600 men had reached the higher ground by late morning. At Gold, Juno and Sword fortified towns were cleared house by house and one battalion came within a few kilometres of Caen."],["The 21st Panzer Division counter-attacks","The 21st Panzer Division attacked between Sword and Juno and nearly reached the Channel. It met stiff resistance from the British 3rd Division and was soon recalled to the area between Caen and Bayeux."]]],
  "Second Battle of El Alamein": ["second-battle-of-el-alamein","45f21e18d9",[["The armies stand in their lines","The Eighth Army stood in the east with XXX Corps in the north, XIII Corps in the south and X Corps ready to exploit. Rommel split his armour into a northern and a southern group, held the 90th Light Division further back and kept the Trieste Division in reserve near the coast."],["The break-in: infantry advance, armour is held up","After a barrage, the four infantry divisions of XXX Corps advanced into the Axis minefields and the armour crawled up behind. Only about half of the infantry reached their objectives and none of the tanks broke through. In the south XIII Corps met determined resistance."],["Axis tanks counter-attack and the Australians strike north","At dusk on 24 October tanks of the northern group advanced from the Kidney feature against the 1st Armoured Division. Over 100 tanks fought and half were destroyed by dark, with neither position altered. The Australians attacked Point 29 at midnight on 25 October."],["Rommel brings his armour north","Rommel reversed his policy of spreading his armour along the front. The 90th Light Division came forward and the 21st Panzer Division went north with a third of the Ariete Division. A shortage of fuel meant the move could not be reversed."],["Operation Supercharge and the charge of the 9th Armoured","Two assault brigades cleared a path from 01:05 and gained most of their objectives. At 06:15 the 9th Armoured Brigade advanced on the Axis gun line and failed to create the gap, though it caused great damage."],["The Axis tanks counter-attack and fail","The remains of the 9th Armoured Brigade came under the 1st Armoured Division, which had deployed. The northern and southern armoured groups counter-attacked at 11:00 and failed under shells and bombs at a cost of about 100 tanks."],["The front is broken and Rommel orders a retreat","British armour burst a 19-kilometre hole in the Axis front. Rommel could wait no longer for Hitler's reply and at 17:30 gave the order to retreat. The two armoured groups pulled back to the west."]]],
  "Second Battle of Panipat": ["second-battle-of-panipat","fbe6db2cd1",[["The armies face each other at Panipat","The Mughals stood with Ali Quli Khan's cavalry in the centre and a wing on each side. Hemu, with the larger army, led from an elephant between his two wings."],["Hemu looses his elephants on the Mughal wings","Hemu began the attack and sent his elephants among the Mughal right and left wings. The Mughal centre advanced and took up a position before a ravine that his elephants and horse could not cross."],["Mughal horsemen turn on the elephants","Men who escaped the elephants veered to the sides and attacked the flanks of Hemu's cavalry with arrows. The Mughal cavalry cut at the elephants' legs and riders, and Hemu pulled his elephants back."],["Ali Quli rides round to the Afghan rear","As the Afghan attack slackened, Ali Quli Khan led his cavalry out, circled round and fell on the Afghan centre from behind. Hemu hurried to meet this charge."],["The Mughal wings are driven back","Hemu kept leading counterattacks, and both Mughal wings were driven back. He moved his elephants and cavalry forward to crush the Mughal centre."],["Hemu is struck by an arrow and his army flees","Hemu was struck in the eye by a chance Mughal arrow and collapsed unconscious. His army panicked, broke formation and fled, and many were killed in flight."]]],
  "Siege of Baghdad (1258)": ["siege-of-baghdad-1258","2a8c64330a",[["The Mongol columns approach Baghdad","Hulegu led the main force, with the Golden Horde princes on the right and Kitbuqa on the left. Baiju crossed the Tigris at Mosul and headed south, while Baghdad held a garrison of its own."],["The dawatdar's army marches out","Baiju's advance party came within about 40 km of the city. The dawatdar met it with 20,000 infantry and forced it to retreat, and the caliphal army pursued."],["The canal dykes are broken and the camp flooded","That night Baiju's forces broke the dykes of the Dujayl Canal and flooded the camp of the celebrating Abbasid army. Many drowned and next morning the rest were routed; only a few, the dawatdar among them, reached Baghdad."],["Hulegu and Kitbuqa close in on the city","Kitbuqa crossed the Tigris to the south and approached the Karkh suburbs, while Hulegu reached the eastern suburbs. The Mongols then enclosed the whole city with a palisade and a moat, completed within a day."],["The assault takes the east wall","The assault began on 29 or 30 January. A first breach on 1 February was driven back, but further breaches over the next two days gave the Mongols the east battlements by 4 February."],["The dawatdar tries to escape by river","The dawatdar sailed down the Tigris to escape. The Mongol preparations on the river held and forced him back into the city with a loss of three ships."],["The caliph surrenders the city","On 7 February a large number of unarmed soldiers and inhabitants came out and were executed. On 10 February the caliph surrendered, bringing his family and 3,000 dignitaries."]]],
  "Siege of Jerusalem (1099)": ["siege-of-jerusalem-1099","4425ed9ad8",[["The crusaders take up positions round the walls","The crusaders could not surround the whole city, so they placed themselves where the walls could be approached. Normandy, Flanders and Godfrey faced the north and north-west, Raymond lay to the south, and the east was left unguarded."],["Raymond moves up to Mount Zion","The valley kept Raymond too far from the walls, so he moved his army up to Mount Zion after two or three days. The army then stood on the hill south-west of the wall."],["The first assault fails","The crusaders attacked the walls with whatever materials they could find. The attack had some early success but failed, which showed that they needed siege engines."],["The armies move into place for the final attack","After almost three weeks of building, the ram and the siege tower were ready. Godfrey moved east behind the ram, the tower came up from Mount Zion, and Iftikhar moved his troops to the north wall."],["The ram and the tower go forward","To the sound of trumpets the crusaders attacked from north and south. Normandy and Flanders shot at the walls, the ram was brought up to the wall, and the tower advanced from Mount Zion while the defenders fired back."],["The ram breaks through but sticks","The ram pierced the wall in the north, but it stuck and the crusaders could not use the breach. They abandoned it, and the general attack was stopped."],["The tower reaches the wall and the crusaders enter","The attack was resumed, and when the tower was pushed up against the wall the crusaders got into the city. Godfrey and Tancred led the way, and the defenders near Mount Zion fought on against Raymond's men."]]],
  "Third Battle of Panipat": ["third-battle-of-panipat","05ba64f017",[["The two lines face each other","The Maratha lines stood a little north of Kala Amb with Bhau and the royal guard in the centre, the Gardis on the left and Holkar and Sindhia on the right. The Afghan line stood to the south with Najib on its left and the Rohillas on its right centre."],["The Gardis advance and the Rohillas fall back","The Maratha left, under Ibrahim Khan, advanced its infantry against the Rohillas. Point-blank salvos sent the Rohillas reeling back to their lines, and the Gardi musketeers held the field for three hours."],["Bhau charges the Afghan left centre","Bhau led a charge against the forces under Shah Wali. The attack nearly broke the Afghan lines, but Shuja-ud-Daulah did not move from his position and the Maratha mounts were exhausted."],["Scindia attacks Najib and the Afghan reserve is called up","The Marathas under Scindia attacked Najib, who fought a defensive action and kept them at bay. Meanwhile Ahmad Shah called up his 15,000 reserve troops from his camp."],["The Afghan reserves charge","Fresh reserves went to support Shah Wali against Bhau in the centre. Some 7,000 Maratha cavalry and infantry were killed before the hand-to-hand fighting began."],["The Gardis are encircled","The Maratha cavalry guarding the Gardis broke its position and went out against the Rohillas. The Rohilla riflemen fired on it, then encircled the Gardis and outflanked the Maratha centre while Shah Wali pressed the front."],["Holkar and Scindia leave the field","Realising the battle was lost, Holkar's and Scindia's contingents merged, and one broke from the Maratha right and escaped south through an opening in the Durrani lines. Bhau and the royal forces fought on."]]]
};
